(window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS=window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS||{})["m1-v21"]={"id":"v21","label":"Version 21 · natural teaching notes with following slides","revision":"ling406-modern-m1-v21-2","scripts":{"m1-lecture01-p001":[],"m1-lecture01-p002":[],"m1-lecture01-p003":[],"m1-lecture01-p004":[],"m1-lecture01-p005":[],"m1-lecture01-p006":[],"m1-lecture01-p007":[{"text":"**Letters are not phonemes**, because letters belong to a writing system while speech sounds belong to the spoken language.","children":["English spelling is especially ambiguous, or irregular as Menn puts it, but even less irregular spelling systems cause problems when languages are compared."]},{"text":"**Pinyin** is a relatively recently invented system for writing Mandarin, and it is perfectly regular, yet an English reader cannot pronounce Wo qu tushuguan (我去图书馆) correctly from English spelling habits.","children":["Pinyin is regular for Mandarin only, so its letters follow Mandarin conventions and not English ones."]},{"text":"**French** spelling is also very regular, and Comment tu t’appelles? still has to be read with French spelling-to-sound conventions.","children":["Matching letters are not evidence of matching sounds, so comparing languages requires describing what speakers actually produce."]}],"m1-lecture01-p008":[{"text":"Comparing languages requires a **standard system** for writing the sounds of the world's languages in a way that accurately captures their **similarities and differences**.","children":["The **International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA)** is that system, because its symbols stand for properties of pronunciation and not for the spelling conventions of any one language."]},{"text":"The IPA **consonant chart** arranges its symbols by how the sounds are produced, so the position of a symbol tells how that sound relates to its neighbors.","children":["The rows have labels such as **plosive**, **nasal** and **fricative**, and the columns have labels such as **bilabial**, **alveolar** and **velar**.","Two sounds can be compared directly even when ordinary spelling writes them differently, and sounds that share a letter can be told apart."]}],"m1-lecture01-p009":[{"text":"The IPA consonant chart is organized by three **features**, which are **place**, **manner** and **voicing**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Place of articulation** is where the vocal tract is constricted, and it runs across the columns from the front of the vocal tract to the back.","children":["Bilabial sounds sit toward the left because their constriction is at the lips, and velar sounds sit farther right."]},{"text":"**Manner of articulation** is the degree of closure, and it runs down the rows from closed to open, so a plosive has a complete closure and a fricative has a narrower passage.","children":["**Voicing** separates the two symbols paired in one position by whether voicing starts before or during the closure or after it, and the right-hand symbol is the voiced one.","The paired p and b sit in the plosive row of the bilabial column, so two consonants can share place and manner and still differ in voicing."]}],"m1-lecture01-p010":[{"text":"Features describe how sounds are alike and different in a relatively **precise and well-defined** way.","children":["/p/ is plus plosive, plus bilabial and minus voiced, while /b/ is plus plosive, plus bilabial and plus voiced."]},{"text":"/p/ and /b/ have the **same manner and place of articulation**, because both are bilabial plosives in which the lips close and then release.","children":["They differ in **voicing**, which is the timing of phonation, meaning vocal-fold vibration, relative to the release of the closure."]},{"text":"The difference between /p/ and /b/ is analogous to the difference between /t/ and /d/, which are made at a different place but have the same voiceless and voiced relation.","children":["Features therefore allow one general statement about **the voiced stops**, such as /b/, /d/ and /g/, in place of a separate description for every consonant."]}],"m1-lecture01-p011":[{"text":"The contrast between **voiced** and **unvoiced** stops is produced differently across languages, and across contexts within one language, and the IPA gives a compact way of writing this.","children":[]},{"text":"In **English**, at the start of a word, the first sound of \"peer\" is often an unvoiced **aspirated** stop [pʰ], and the first sound of \"beer\" is often an unvoiced, **unaspirated** stop [p].","children":["**Aspiration** is the puff of air after the closure is released, so a hand held in front of the mouth feels the difference between \"peer\" and \"beer\".","The superscript h in [pʰ] marks aspiration, so [pʰ] and [p] are both minus voiced and differ only in aspiration."]},{"text":"In most varieties of **French**, the contrast is really between [p] for \"pou\" and voiced [b] for \"boue\".","children":["[pʰ], [p] and [b] are three different phones, even though a language may sort them into fewer contrasting categories."]}],"m1-lecture01-p012":[{"text":"**Slashes** mark **broad transcription**, which writes the **phonemes** of an utterance, as in /p/ and /b/.","children":["Broad transcription keeps only the distinctions that organize the words of the language."]},{"text":"**Square brackets** mark **narrow transcription**, which includes as much detail as possible about how the sound was actually produced, as in [pʰ], [p] and [b].","children":["Narrow transcription keeps differences such as aspiration even when they do not distinguish words, so recording more detail does not create more phonemes."]},{"text":"There are two broad labels and three narrow forms here, so a **phone**, which is a specific produced sound, and a **phoneme**, which is a sound category that distinguishes words in a language, are not interchangeable terms.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p013":[{"text":"**Phonetic features**, especially for consonants, are **anchored to how speech is produced**, so each place label names a physical constriction in the vocal tract.","children":[]},{"text":"A **bilabial** closure brings the upper and lower lips together at the front of the mouth.","children":["An **alveolar** constriction raises the tongue tip toward the ridge behind the upper teeth.","A **velar** constriction raises the back of the tongue toward the soft palate."]},{"text":"Place is separate from manner, because a complete closure can be made at several different places.","children":["Finding the place of a consonant means locating the main constriction and not relying on the written letter."]}],"m1-lecture01-p014":[{"text":"Consonant features describe both the **route of the airflow** and the **articulators** that make the constriction.","children":[]},{"text":"A **nasal** consonant closes the mouth at some point while the passage to the nose stays open, so the air goes out through the nose.","children":["A nasal can share its place in the mouth with a stop, as when the lips are closed, and differ only in the route that the air takes."]},{"text":"A **labiodental** constriction brings the lower lip against the upper teeth, while a **dental** constriction puts the tongue at the front teeth.","children":["Both are made at the front of the mouth, so the difference is whether the lower lip or the tongue approaches the teeth."]}],"m1-lecture01-p015":[{"text":"Vowels are described by **height**, by **front or back** tongue position, and by **lip rounding**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Close** vowels tend to have a higher jaw position than **open** vowels, and the tongue is higher as well.","children":["The tongue is generally farther forward in the mouth for **front** vowels than for **back** vowels.","**Round** vowels are made with rounding of the lips, and where two vowel symbols are paired the right-hand one is the rounded vowel."]},{"text":"/y/ is the vowel in the French word \"tu\" and the Mandarin word \"qu\" (去), and it sounds like /u/ to English speakers.","children":["Making an /i/ and then rounding the lips produces /y/, so /y/ is a close front vowel like /i/ that differs in rounding, and it does not use the back tongue position of /u/."]}],"m1-lecture01-p016":[{"text":"A **dot** marks the **center of the tongue body** for each vowel, so vowels can be compared by where that center sits in the mouth.","children":["The colored outlines trace the whole tongue shape for each vowel, and those shapes differ in more ways than the position of one dot."]},{"text":"Vowels are more complicated than one fixed tongue position, because some vowels are very **dynamic**.","children":["The **diphthongs** in \"boat\" and \"bait\" change while they are being produced, so each is a movement through time and not one held position."]},{"text":"The whole vocal tract contributes to how a vowel is produced, so a single point is a useful reference and still an incomplete description of the vowel.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p017":[{"text":"Vowels are very noticeably different across **dialects**, even within American English.","children":[]},{"text":"A speaker of the mid-Atlantic dialect, more specifically from the outer boroughs of New York City, distinguishes between the two vowels in the lower right corner of the vowel chart.","children":["Those two vowels are the rounded back vowel ɔ and the low back vowel ɑ.","For that speaker \"caught\" and \"cot\" have different vowels, so they are **not homophones**, meaning they do not sound the same."]},{"text":"Most people from other parts of the country do not make this distinction, so for them \"caught\" and \"cot\" sound alike.","children":["A vowel inventory belongs to a particular dialect, and the difference is a systematic dialect pattern and not a pronunciation mistake."]}],"m1-lecture01-p018":[{"text":"A listener understands people who say \"Aaron\" and \"Erin\" with the **same vowel**, even when the listener's own dialect keeps those two vowels apart.","children":["Other listeners can hear a speaker produce the two different vowels for some time and only notice the difference once attention is drawn to it."]},{"text":"Both facts are a first encounter with the **lack of invariance problem**, which is that one speech category has different acoustic forms across speakers and contexts, so no single fixed acoustic value identifies it.","children":["When vowels are plotted by the frequency of the first formant and the frequency of the second formant, each vowel spreads over a wide region and neighboring vowels overlap."]},{"text":"Listeners still recover a stable interpretation from this variable signal, and how they do so is a problem that comes up again and again in the study of speech.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p019":[{"text":"**Minimal pairs** are important for establishing **contrast**.","children":["A minimal pair is two different words whose difference in meaning depends on a difference in one speech sound in the same context."]},{"text":"Two **phones** are **contrastive** when a minimal pair can be found in which the difference between them results in a **difference in meaning**, and contrastive phones are examples of **different phonemes**.","children":[]},{"text":"Establishing that a speaker really has two different low back vowels required finding two words, \"caught\" and \"cot\", whose distinction depends on the difference between those vowels.","children":["The surrounding consonants are the same in both words, so the vowel carries the contrast.","For a speaker who merges the two vowels, this pair does not establish two vowel phonemes."]}],"m1-lecture01-p020":[{"text":"Minimal pairs also give evidence that **phonemic contrasts depend on a particular feature**.","children":[]},{"text":"In English the pairs /b/ and /p/, /t/ and /d/, and /k/ and /g/ all differ on **voicing**.","children":["Each pair keeps place and stop manner the same and changes the voicing category, so the same feature makes the contrast at the bilabial, alveolar and velar places."]},{"text":"This is known from comparing **minimal pairs in each language**, so the evidence has to come from the language being analyzed.","children":["Two phones existing physically is not enough, because a sound difference is a phonemic contrast only where it separates words in that language."]}],"m1-lecture01-p021":[{"text":"There is a great deal of **variability** in how phonemes are realized, and some of that variability is very **predictable**.","children":[]},{"text":"The different phones that are different realizations of the **same phoneme** are called **allophones**.","children":["A speaker produces /p/ differently depending on its position and the neighboring sounds, without changing which word is said."]},{"text":"Recording the phrases \"pot that\", \"spot that\" and \"stop that\" in Praat puts /p/ in three environments, which are the start of the word, the position after /s/, and the end of the word.","children":["The release and aspiration of /p/ can then be compared across the three phrases while the consonant stays a realization of /p/."]}],"m1-lecture01-p022":[{"text":"A recording of \"pot that\", \"spot that\" and \"stop that\" makes the **allophones of /p/** observable in time.","children":["The timing of the closure, the release, the aspiration and the following voiced sound can differ across the three contexts, and all three words still contain the same phoneme /p/."]},{"text":"The upper display is a **waveform**, which gives the amplitude of the sound signal over time.","children":[]},{"text":"The lower display is a **spectrogram**, which gives the distribution of acoustic energy over time and frequency, with time running left to right and frequency running upward.","children":["The two displays line up in time, so an abrupt release or a quiet interval can be matched from one to the other."]}],"m1-lecture01-p023":[{"text":"The word \"stop\" is sometimes said as [stɑpʰ], with a released and aspirated /p/, often when the word is stressed or when someone is trying to speak clearly in **citation form**.","children":[]},{"text":"There is **no difference in meaning** between [stɑp̚] and [stɑpʰ], and no minimal pair can be found for these two phones in these phonological contexts in English.","children":["The two phones are called **allophones** for that reason, where allo means different, so they are different phones for the same phoneme.","The small mark in [p̚] means the stop is unreleased, and the superscript h in [pʰ] means it is aspirated."]},{"text":"These forms also **alternate in different morphological forms**, such as \"stop\", \"stops\", \"stopped\" and \"stopping\".","children":["Adding material after the /p/ changes its environment, so whether the /p/ is released and aspirated depends on the form in which the word occurs."]}],"m1-lecture01-p024":[{"text":"The word **voicing** is used in two different ways, which can be confusing.","children":[]},{"text":"In **narrow transcription**, which describes specific **phones**, a sound is called voiced if the vocal folds vibrate during the whole gesture or sound.","children":[]},{"text":"In **broad transcription**, which describes **phonemes**, voicing is a contrast that is **realized in a number of different ways**.","children":["One way is the presence or absence of aspiration at the beginnings of words, and another is the relative duration of voicing to closure in \"writer\" and \"rider\".","A phoneme can be called voiced without vocal-fold vibration through its whole closure, so it matters whether a statement is about a phone or about a phoneme."]}],"m1-lecture01-p025":[{"text":"**Phonotactics** is, roughly, which phonemes can occur together in syllable structures.","children":[]},{"text":"This is clearest with **clusters** that cannot occur in English words.","children":["English speakers say the name Sbarro as [spɑroʊ] or [zbɑroʊ] but never [sbɑroʊ], whatever they think they are saying."]},{"text":"When forced to deal with clusters that English phonotactics does not allow, as in foreign names like Zbigniew (Polish) or Gbagbo (Kru), English speakers tend to **insert vowels** and say [zɨ’bIg.nʲu] or [gə.bɑ.gə.boʊ].","children":["The inserted vowels break the cluster into extra syllables, so the name fits English syllable patterns.","Rules about syllable structure turn out to be complex, and they seem to shape speech production."]}],"m1-lecture01-p026":[{"text":"**Phonemes are not letters**, and they are not even specific sounds or gestures.","children":["A **phoneme** is the smallest phonetic unit in a language that can convey a **distinction in meaning**, so it is defined by the contrasts it makes.","One phoneme can have several allophones and still be one category, which is part of why this is complicated in practice."]},{"text":"The **International Phonetic Alphabet** gives speech scientists a way of communicating about the similarities and differences between sounds across languages.","children":[]},{"text":"It is easy to think of the IPA as an **alphabet of phonemes**, but that would be a mistake because phonemes are not letters.","children":["A letter belongs to a writing system, a phone is one produced speech sound, and a phoneme is a category that separates meanings."]}],"m1-lecture01-p027":[{"text":"Literate people easily imagine that speech is produced as an **orderly stream of letter-like units**, but speech is nothing like that.","children":[]},{"text":"A **spectrogram** of the sentence \"A sore back is a real pain\" has frequency patterns that change continuously over time, with transitions connecting neighboring sounds.","children":["Time runs from left to right, frequency increases upward, and darker regions have stronger acoustic energy.","The phonetic labels and boundary markers above it are annotations, so they organize the signal without cutting it into separate pieces."]},{"text":"This is why **phonemes are not letters**, because a transcription is a row of discrete symbols while the sound it describes is one **continuous** signal in which neighboring sounds overlap.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p028":[{"text":"The **articulators** in action are the **lips, jaw, tongue** and other structures of the vocal tract, and they move in a coordinated, continuous way during speech.","children":["The tongue changes the shape of the space inside the mouth, while the lips and jaw change its opening, so together they shape the outgoing sound."]},{"text":"A side view of the vocal tract from USC SPAN locates the tongue inside the mouth and the passage leading toward the throat.","children":[]},{"text":"Because the movements run together as one ongoing sequence, a speech gesture is part of a **moving system** and is not one fixed posture repeated unchanged for every occurrence of a phoneme.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p029":[{"text":"**Hockett** gave an analogy for why hearing speech is not reading off a row of separate units.","children":["He imagined a row of unboiled Easter eggs of various sizes and colors on a moving belt, which carries them between the two rollers of a wringer that smashes them and rubs them into each other."]},{"text":"The row of eggs before the wringer stands for the **series of impulses from the phoneme source**, and the mess that comes out stands for the **output of the speech transmitter**.","children":[]},{"text":"An inspector examines the passing mess and decides, from the broken and unbroken yolks, the spread-out albumen and the colored bits of shell, which eggs arrived at the wringer.","children":["The **inspector stands for the hearer**, who only has to **identify** the eggs and does not have to put them together again, which would be physically impossible."]}],"m1-lecture01-p030":[{"text":"Some people have started to question whether **phonemes are \"real\"**, either **psychologically** or **linguistically**.","children":["A phonemic description is useful for stating contrasts, but that does not settle how a speaker stores words or how a listener recognizes speech."]},{"text":"**Lotto and Holt** wrote about the illusion of the phoneme, and **Beckman and Edwards** wrote about how phonological categories develop and the primacy of lexical learning.","children":["**Port** asked how words are stored in memory and looked beyond phones and phonemes, and **Goldinger and Azuma** wrote about speech perception."]},{"text":"What these works share is the question of whether phoneme-sized units are what the mind actually uses in learning, memory and perception.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p031":[],"m1-lecture01-p032":[],"m1-lecture03-p001":[],"m1-lecture03-p002":[],"m1-lecture03-p003":[],"m1-lecture03-p004":[{"text":"In a Scrabble argument, one player says \"knifes\" is not a word because the plural of the noun knife is \"knives\", and the other answers that Tony \"knifes\" Bernardo in West Side Story.","children":[]},{"text":"Both players are right, because **a word form depends on its grammatical role**, so \"knives\" is the plural of the noun while \"knifes\" is a present-tense form of the verb.","children":["Making the verb knife from the noun with no added affix is **zero derivation**, a kind of **derivational morphology**, which builds new words.","Adding the ending for the plural or for the verb is **inflectional morphology**, which marks the form the grammar requires."]},{"text":"The sentence \"He knifes him\" is a **clause** made of a singular noun subject, a verb and an object noun, so the subject requires the **third person singular** form of the verb.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p005":[{"text":"**Derivational morphology** is a process that derives new words from existing parts, and those parts, or units, are called **morphemes**.","children":["A morpheme is not a syllable or a single speech sound, because what makes it a unit is that it contributes meaning or grammatical information."]},{"text":"The word \"morphology\" has a transparent derivation, because morph- comes from the Greek for form and -ology, also from Greek, means something like the study of, so together they give **the study of the forms of words**.","children":["A similar process gives \"morpheme\", and \"phoneme\" is built on phone, another morpheme from the Greek, for speech."]},{"text":"By that pattern a unit in text should get a name built the same way, and \"graphology\" should be the study of such units. That guess goes wrong, because the meaning of a derived word is only partly predictable from its parts.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p006":[{"text":"Derivational morphology is **productive**, meaning it can keep making new words, but only in **limited ways**, as trying to make up new words and guess their meanings makes clear.","children":["The meaning of the final form is **only partially predictable** from its base, so it rests partly on convention."]},{"text":"Even **zero derivation**, which changes the part of speech of a word without changing its form, usually works with a **narrow sense of the base word**.","children":["Top-ranked image search results for \"knife\" include different cutting tools and tableware, and a search for \"prison shiv\" gives an improvised weapon.","The noun covers a range of objects, but to \"knife\" somebody names one narrow action, so those objects are not equally natural choices for it even though they resemble one another as cutting tools."]}],"m1-lecture03-p007":[{"text":"Derivational morphology is also limited in **how widely and how regularly affixes are applied**. The suffix **-hood** added to a stem noun gives a state of being associated with that noun, as in \"brotherhood\" and \"sisterhood\".","children":["\"Knighthood\" is a little odd, because two brothers share their brotherhood, but each knight has his own knighthood."]},{"text":"The pattern has gaps and exceptions, so a recognizable affix does not combine equally well with every sensible base.","children":["\"Neighborhood\" is a perfectly good word, but there -hood does a different job entirely.","The form friendhood is not used because English says friendship instead, for no apparent reason. The form enemyhood maybe ought to be a word, since the only words for that idea are top-shelf ones like enmity or antagonism."]}],"m1-lecture03-p008":[{"text":"**Zero derivation** changes the category of a word without adding a visible affix, yet even it does complicated things with **semantics**, the meaning of the word.","children":[]},{"text":"Dish, spoon, fork and table are strong associates of the noun knife, but their meanings as verbs are **not similar** to one another or to the verb knife.","children":["In \"She can dish it out but she can’t take it\" and \"They decided to table the issue after a day of inconclusive negotiations\", the verbs have learned meanings that do not come from naming the object.","\"He was born with a silver spoon and a forked tongue in his mouth\" relies on established figurative meanings, the kind studied in **Cognitive Linguistics**."]},{"text":"An unchanged form does not mean an unchanged meaning, because **conventional usage** decides what the converted word communicates in its sentence.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p009":[{"text":"More commonly, derivational morphology changes the **pronunciation and spelling** of a word, for instance by adding **affixes**.","children":["In \"certificate\" /sər.ˈtI.fɨ.kɨt/, \"certification\" /ˌsər.tɨ.fɨ.ˈkeI.ʃən/ and \"recertification\" /ˈri.sər.tɨ.fɨ.ˌkeI.ʃən/, each added affix moves the **stress** and changes vowels in the rest of the word."]},{"text":"Adding an affix can change the **phonology** of the rest of the word, which is one reason for \"The Chaos\" of English spelling and why higher fidelity to the spoken form does not necessarily improve readability.","children":[]},{"text":"Stress is also the major change between the noun and verb forms of \"record\" and \"invite\", as in \"He did not receive an invite\" and \"We did not invite him\".","children":["The two words spelled \"entrance\" also differ in stress, but they are not actually related to each other."]}],"m1-lecture03-p010":[{"text":"**Inflectional morphology** changes the meaning of words in a **more predictable** way than derivation does, because it gives grammatical forms of the same word.","children":["**Plurals** include \"knives\" from the noun knife, \"two forks\" and \"a lot of mice\".","The **third person singular present** gives \"knifes\", \"she walks\" and \"he runs\", and the **past** gives \"knifed\", \"walked\" and \"sang\"."]},{"text":"Menn puts this succinctly, writing that we would think it silly to have separate dictionary entries for the singular and plural forms of nouns.","children":[]},{"text":"The change in meaning is predictable, but the **phonological process is often irregular**, as in \"mice\" and \"sang\" beside \"forks\" and \"walked\".","children":["That irregularity matters for different language learning models and for kinds of developmental language impairment."]}],"m1-lecture03-p011":[{"text":"**Inflectional morphology** also depends on the **syntax** of the rest of the sentence, so the grammar around a word can require a particular inflected form.","children":["In the phrase \"two [noun]+pl\", whatever noun fills the slot must be pluralized somehow, whether regularly as in \"two forks\" or irregularly as in \"knives\"."]},{"text":"There are **no syntactic processes like this** for even very productive derivational forms, because no position in a sentence requires a derived word.","children":["The closest case is a formula like \"Once you [verb] it, you can un-[verb] it\", but that is **not syntax**, since nothing in the grammar requires every verb to have an un- form."]}],"m1-lecture03-p012":[{"text":"English is **pretty limited in its use of inflection**, and its familiar cases are plurals on nouns and the third person singular present and the past on verbs.","children":["The forms are few, such as \"knives\", \"knifes\" and \"knifed\" for knife."]},{"text":"Other languages put more grammatical information into the forms of words.","children":["Many languages conjugate verbs much more elaborately than English for **person** and for **tense**.","Many languages have different ways of forming the plural for **small and large numbers**."]},{"text":"Many languages also have **case markings** for nouns, which encode information about the syntactic or semantic role of the noun.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p013":[{"text":"Both **inflectional** and **derivational morphology** create new phonological forms out of old parts, and both change the meanings of base forms.","children":[]},{"text":"**Derivational morphology creates new words**, as when the verb knife is made from the noun.","children":["It is productive, but not always, and typically in limited ways, so its results can have partly conventional meanings."]},{"text":"**Inflectional morphology changes how words interact syntactically with other words**, as when \"knifes\" is the form needed after \"he\".","children":["It is highly productive but not always regular, and it is **required by syntax**.","Regularity of sound cannot decide between the two, so the test is whether a change makes a new word or marks the form a sentence needs."]}],"m1-lecture03-p014":[{"text":"Nouns and verbs are kinds of **content words**, and other content words include modifiers such as adjectives and adverbs.","children":[]},{"text":"**Function words**, such as determiners and prepositions, organize how content words relate, so they only come into play once more than a couple of words are put together into **phrases, clauses and sentences**.","children":[]},{"text":"In \"{The video for “More than Words”} came out before {most of you} were born\", the curly brackets mark two **noun phrases**.","children":["The subject is the whole first phrase and not only the noun video.","The two phrases differ in length, so a phrase is not defined by a fixed number of words."]}],"m1-lecture03-p015":[{"text":"\"The video for “More than Words”\" is a **noun phrase** (NP) whose structure is a **determiner**, then a **noun**, then a **prepositional phrase**, written Det + N + PP.","children":["The **function words** here are \"The\", a determiner that begins the noun phrase, and \"for\", a preposition.","The preposition begins the prepositional phrase that allows the song title to specify which video is meant."]},{"text":"The noun video is the head, so the whole phrase can act as one unit in a sentence.","children":[]},{"text":"The title “More than Words” is not really part of the sentence but is used like a noun, because without the quotation marks it is only a bunch of words strung together in a row and not a phrase.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p016":[{"text":"The description Det + N + PP for a noun phrase is **incredibly abstract**, because phrases with very different words and lengths have the **same structure**.","children":["\"two guys from the early ‘nineties\" is the determiner \"two\", the head noun \"guys\" and a prepositional phrase beginning with \"from\".","\"a band with only one hit\" is the determiner \"a\", the head noun \"band\" and a prepositional phrase beginning with \"with\"."]},{"text":"In \"one and only one phrase unrelated to early ‘nineties one-hit wonder Extreme\", the determiner is the several words \"one and only one\" and the head noun is \"phrase\".","children":["Its modifier begins with the adjective \"unrelated\" followed by a \"to\" phrase, so the broad pattern of determiner, head noun and modifier holds while the inside of the modifier differs."]}],"m1-lecture03-p017":[{"text":"This level of **abstraction** is needed because, without some way of abstracting away from the individual words, very different surface forms could not be described as similar.","children":["It would also be impossible to talk coherently about similarities and differences between languages."]},{"text":"All languages have **NPs**, but they can **form them very differently**, and the Chinese NP 咖啡店裡面的小貓, glossed as coffeeshop inside + cat, means the cat in the coffee shop and has the order PP then N.","children":["English puts \"in the coffee shop\" after \"cat\", while Chinese puts the location before the noun."]},{"text":"The Chinese NP also has **no determiner**, which helps explain why determiners are so difficult for speakers of Chinese languages to learn in a second language.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p018":[{"text":"The prepositional phrases seen so far share one general structure, because each is a **preposition followed by a noun phrase**, written **P + NP**.","children":[]},{"text":"\"from the early ‘nineties\" is the preposition \"from\" and the NP \"the early ‘nineties\", and that NP is itself Det + Adj + N.","children":["Inside it, \"the\" is the determiner, \"early\" is the adjective and \"‘nineties\" is the noun."]},{"text":"\"with only one hit\" is \"with\" and the NP \"only one hit\", and \"unrelated to\" is treated as the P before the NP \"early ‘nineties one-hit wonder Extreme\".","children":["The NPs differ inside, but each counts as a single unit after the preposition, so one short label can stand for a larger inner structure."]}],"m1-lecture03-p019":[{"text":"The curly brackets and parentheses in \"from + { the + early + ‘nineties }\" are clumsy because what they try to represent is really **hierarchical**, meaning units sit inside other units.","children":["The determiner, adjective and noun are all inside the **noun phrase**, which in turn is inside the **prepositional phrase**.","The prepositional phrase is then inside a sentence, if it is not part of yet another noun phrase."]},{"text":"This nesting is part of why people started using **trees** to diagram sentences, because a tree puts each unit above the parts it contains.","children":["Hierarchy is also an **important feature of languages**, since words that sit side by side can belong to different levels of structure."]}],"m1-lecture03-p020":[{"text":"A **verb phrase** (VP) is a verb together with the material that completes it. In \"The video for “More than Words” came out before most of you were born\", the VP is \"came out\" and the PP \"before most of you were born\", so its structure is **V + PP**.","children":["That PP is hard to unpack, because \"most of you were born\" looks like a verb phrase, but it specifies a point in time that complements \"before\"."]},{"text":"Many verb phrases also contain **noun phrases**, so \"sang the song\" is V + NP, a verb followed by the noun phrase \"the song\".","children":["\"worked himself to death\" and \"threw a party for him\" are both V + NP + PP, with \"to death\" and \"for him\" as the PPs."]}],"m1-lecture03-p021":[{"text":"The sentence divides into the NP \"The video for “More than Words”\", which goes Det + N + PP, and the VP \"came out before most of you were born\", which goes V + PP.","children":[]},{"text":"A **clause** is defined as a **VP and its subject**, and the **subject** is defined as the NP that **controls the verb**.","children":["Here the whole first NP is the subject, and its **head noun** is \"video\".","The song title sits inside the modifying PP, so it helps identify the video without becoming the subject."]},{"text":"Because there is one VP with one subject, **the whole sentence is one clause**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p022":[{"text":"\"{The plums that were in the icebox} {were delicious}\" is a simple **one-clause sentence** made of a subject NP and a VP.","children":["A subject **controls** its verb, which means the verb **agrees** with \"plums\" and not \"icebox\", because \"plums\" is the **head** of the noun phrase."]},{"text":"\"The plums in the icebox, which you were probably saving for breakfast, were delicious\" has a **clause within a clause**, set off with commas.","children":["\"you\" is the subject of \"were probably saving for breakfast\", and \"plums\" is the subject of \"were delicious\".","Changing \"you\" to \"my roommate\" changes only the inner verb, to \"was probably saving\", because each subject controls its own verb."]},{"text":"In \"Forgive me\", the subject is **implicit**, since the speaker wants whoever shares his icebox to forgive him for eating the plums.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p023":[{"text":"\"I have eaten {the plums in the icebox}\" and \"The plums in the icebox were eaten {by me}\" **mean the same thing**, but they are **syntactically very different**.","children":[]},{"text":"In the first sentence, the **subject** \"I\" comes first and is followed by a verb phrase, and the bracketed NP inside that verb phrase is the **object** of the verb.","children":[]},{"text":"In the second sentence, \"the plums in the icebox\" becomes the **subject**.","children":["The evidence is agreement, because the verb \"were eaten\" agrees with the plural noun \"plums\".","\"by me\" is only a **PP** fleshing out the VP, so it names who did the eating without being the subject."]}],"m1-lecture03-p024":[{"text":"It can seem counterintuitive that \"me\" is not the subject of \"The plums in the icebox were eaten {by me}\", because English usually aligns the **syntactic subject** of a sentence with its **agent**, the one who does the action.","children":["Telling **who did what to whom** is the job of **semantic roles**, more so than syntactic ones."]},{"text":"In that passive sentence the order is **Undergoer**, **Location**, Verb, **Agent**, with \"The plums\" as undergoer, \"in the icebox\" as location and \"by me\" as agent.","children":["In \"I have eaten the plums in the icebox\" the order is Agent, Verb, Undergoer, Location."]},{"text":"**The semantic roles do not change** when the syntax changes, so only syntactic roles such as subject and object move.","children":["How different relationships between syntactic and semantic roles influence processing is a question for **sentence processing**."]}],"m1-lecture03-p025":[{"text":"Language is **highly generative**, which means familiar parts can be combined into new words and new sentences, and **derivational morphology** is the part that makes new words.","children":[]},{"text":"**Syntax** organizes words into clauses, clauses into phrases, and phrases into sentences, and **inflectional morphology** modifies words for different syntactic roles.","children":[]},{"text":"People do all of this **mostly without thinking explicitly** about it.","children":["That is why one of the hardest things to understand about Linguistics is **what there is to explain**, because some of the most complex phenomena are hiding in plain sight."]}],"m1-lecture03-p026":[],"m1-lecture03-p027":[],"m1-lecture04-p001":[],"m1-lecture04-p002":[{"text":"**Association norms** are records of the words people give in response to a cue word. A participant sees a word for a few seconds and writes down the first few words that come to mind.","children":["The task asks for immediate associations and not for a dictionary definition."]},{"text":"Collecting answers from many people is called **norming**, and it reveals which associations come up again and again and which are unusual.","children":["The same cue can bring out several different associates across participants, so the norm is the whole pattern of responses to that cue."]}],"m1-lecture04-p003":[{"text":"LION is an **association cue**, a word presented so that the participant writes the first words it brings to mind.","children":["The responses reveal **connections in the participant's lexical knowledge**, which is their stored knowledge of words."]},{"text":"A response such as TIGER would connect **two animal concepts**, but it is only one possible associate and not an answer that every participant must give.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p004":[{"text":"TRIPE is an association cue whose **form resembles STRIPES**, because the two words share a sequence of letters.","children":[]},{"text":"The shared letters do not give the words a shared meaning, so **similarity in form** and a **connection in meaning** are different relationships.","children":["This pair makes the **arbitrariness** of the relationship between form and meaning concrete, because overlapping letters occur in words with very different meanings."]}],"m1-lecture04-p005":[{"text":"FREE is another cue in the association task. The participant **generates** the words that come to mind from it and does not choose from a list of answers.","children":[]},{"text":"Writing the response down makes an **internal association** available to compare across people, which is what a norm requires.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p006":[{"text":"STRIPES is a cue that names a **visual property**. An association can connect a property with an object or animal that has it, so associated words do not have to name the same kind of thing.","children":[]},{"text":"STRIPES and TIGER can be **associated** even though one names a pattern and the other names an animal.","children":["That link differs from the **similarity** between two animal concepts."]}],"m1-lecture04-p007":[{"text":"PEAR is an association cue whose **form overlaps with BEAR**, although the two words have different meanings.","children":[]},{"text":"The resemblance in sound or spelling does not make the fruit and the animal the same concept, so a **relationship of form** is separate from a **relationship of meaning**.","children":["A shared sound or spelling pattern is not evidence that two words have similar meanings."]}],"m1-lecture04-p008":[{"text":"BEAR is another cue for a freely generated association, and its form overlaps with PEAR. Words can **resemble one another in form** without resembling one another in meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"Association norms record the responses people **actually give**, so the experimenter does not have to decide ahead of time whether a response will be based on form or on meaning.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p009":[{"text":"TREE is a cue for the association task, and the participant's first responses are evidence about which words are **readily connected** with it in that task.","children":[]},{"text":"The cue stays the same physical word while different people supply **different associates**.","children":["The norm sums up all of those responses, so no single response counts as the only association the word has."]}],"m1-lecture04-p010":[{"text":"TIGER connects with LION through their **similar meanings** as animals, and it connects with STRIPES through a **characteristic pattern**.","children":["Two animal names are related through shared category membership, while an animal and one of its properties are related in a different way."]},{"text":"Because TIGER has both kinds of link, **association and similarity are not interchangeable**, and both can contribute to the structure of a lexical network.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p011":[],"m1-lecture04-p012":[{"text":"Three cues are critical for what comes later, and they are **LION, TIGER and STRIPES**. LION and TIGER name **similar animals**, while TIGER and STRIPES connect an animal with a **characteristic property**.","children":["An association is measured by counting how often participants give one word in response to another."]},{"text":"The pairs TRIPE and STRIPES, and PEAR and BEAR, make a different point about the **arbitrariness of the relationship between form and meaning**.","children":["Their forms overlap but their meanings do not, because the relationship between the sound or spelling of a word and its meaning is largely arbitrary.","Similarity of form, similarity of meaning and the tendency of one word to bring another to mind are three separate things to measure."]}],"m1-lecture04-p013":[{"text":"**Associated** means that one item tends to bring another to mind, while **similar** means that two items share properties.","children":["TIGER and STRIPES are associated without being two similar objects."]},{"text":"The answers people give also depend on the **task context**. Participants may use a deliberate strategy, and the other words in the list can influence which ideas come to mind easily.","children":["A list of only wild animals could encourage different responses from a list of only household appliances."]},{"text":"A norm is for that reason evidence about responses **under particular conditions**, so reading one includes asking how the cue was presented and what other cues were present.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p014":[{"text":"The chapter title \"How Brains Work\" is better read as some **basic metaphors from neuroscience** that are useful in psycholinguistics, because they are the basis for most theory and experimental design in the field.","children":[]},{"text":"There are five of them. **Activation** means a representation can become active, and **spreading of activation** means it passes activity to connected representations over weighted connections.","children":["A **distributed representation** encodes an item as a pattern across many units.","**Associative learning**, such as Hebbian learning, changes the relationships between units that are active together."]},{"text":"**Bottom-up processing** runs from sensory features toward words, and **top-down processing** runs from word knowledge toward the interpretation of features.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p015":[{"text":"**Hubel and Wiesel** recorded from **simple cells** in **primary visual cortex**, which respond to lines of particular orientations in a particular **receptive field**.","children":["A receptive field is the part of the sensory input that a cell responds to."]},{"text":"They compared a cell's activity as the orientation and movement of a line changed. One cell **activated most strongly** to upward movement of a line at an angle of about three hundred and fifteen degrees, and it fired far less in the other conditions.","children":["Because the cell responds selectively, its electrochemical activity carries information about that **combination of features**, so the cell can be described as **representing** it."]},{"text":"This work on relating neural activity to sensory input came at the same time as the Cognitive Revolution.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p016":[{"text":"A cell from further up the **visual processing stream** responds to something more complex than line segments. In data adapted from **Gross and colleagues**, a neuron in monkey **inferotemporal cortex** fires strongly to **near-profile views** of a monkey's face and stays near its baseline rate for frontal and rear views.","children":["Its response is **graded**, since it fires a little to some views and much more to others."]},{"text":"It seems natural to describe this cell as **representing** something complex, the profile of another monkey.","children":[]},{"text":"**Activation** in psycholinguistics extends this idea as a metaphor to **perceptual and conceptual representations**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p017":[{"text":"Imagine a very simple set of **lexical representations** for the words lion, tiger and stripes. Each one is a **node** in a network, and a node is a unit that can be **activated by a specific word**.","children":["The printed word LION supplies perceptual evidence, while the lion node stands for the word the system could recognize from that evidence."]},{"text":"Treating words as nodes makes it possible to say how much activity each one receives, so the model can describe how evidence for one word changes the state of the whole system.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p018":[{"text":"The printed word can appear as LION, lion or Lion, in different typefaces and sizes, and every one of these has to activate the **same lion node**.","children":["The physical display changes while the identity of the word stays the same."]},{"text":"This is the **lack of invariance problem**, which also arises with phonemes, and it means the sensory form of a linguistic unit varies even though the unit stays constant.","children":["A full account would have to say how complicated the **receptive field** of each node must be to handle that variation. For now it is enough to imagine nodes that words can activate."]}],"m1-lecture04-p019":[{"text":"**Activation can be partial**, so a node does not have to be either fully active or fully inactive.","children":[]},{"text":"When the word LION **contrasts poorly with a noise mask**, the input gives only weak evidence for that word, so the lion node receives only **weak activation**.","children":["The word is not yet identified at that point, because the weak input raises the node's activity without giving enough support for a confident identification.","A graded value lets the model describe the intermediate states of processing as useful evidence emerges from the noise."]}],"m1-lecture04-p020":[{"text":"As the stimulus becomes clearer, **activation increases**. LION becomes easier to tell apart from the noise, so the input gives more evidence to the lion node and the node becomes more active.","children":[]},{"text":"Two changes go together here. The physical change is a better relation between the word signal and the noise mask, and the change in the model is **stronger activity** in the representation of the word.","children":["Keeping the two apart explains how a manipulation of the display can influence processing."]}],"m1-lecture04-p021":[{"text":"At some point the **signal-to-noise ratio**, which is the strength of the useful word information relative to the interfering background, becomes very favorable. Activity in the lion node is then strong enough to say that the simple network has **identified** the word \"lion\".","children":["The tiger and stripes nodes remain as alternatives that were not identified."]},{"text":"This is the **perceptual identification task**, in which participants push a button as soon as they can identify the item.","children":["The measure is **how long identification takes**, so a condition that makes identification easier leads to an earlier button press."]}],"m1-lecture04-p022":[{"text":"Two kinds of factor can influence how quickly a word is identified in the perceptual identification task.","children":[]},{"text":"**Features of the stimulus itself** concern the target word, and **features of the experimental environment** concern the surrounding situation, including what the participant has just encountered.","children":["Keeping them apart makes a testable comparison possible, because the same target can be presented in different contexts, or different targets can be compared under the same task conditions."]},{"text":"Identification speed for that reason depends on more than visibility alone.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p023":[{"text":"Two **features of the stimulus itself** influence how quickly a word is identified. One is **word frequency**, which is how often the word is encountered, and the other is **how similar it is to other words**.","children":["A frequently encountered word can be easier to activate."]},{"text":"Two **features of the experimental environment** also matter. Having just seen the same word produces **repetition priming**, and having just seen a similar or related word can produce other kinds of priming.","children":["Frequency concerns experience built up with a word, while repetition priming concerns its recent presentation, although both make a representation easier to reach.","A related prime changes the context without repeating the target."]}],"m1-lecture04-p024":[{"text":"Most models of this kind assume that activation **unfolds over time**, and **activation dynamics** is the name for how the activity of a representation changes over time.","children":["In the Interactive Activation model of **Rumelhart and McClelland**, activation for the word CAVE rises above the competing words while the input supports it and then gradually falls."]},{"text":"**Frequency effects** can be explained by assuming that activation dynamics are **tuned up by experience**. Words that are encountered more often become more sensitive, so they are easier to activate.","children":["Different computational models simulate frequency effects in different ways."]}],"m1-lecture04-p025":[{"text":"**Repetition priming** is faster identification of a word that is seen again very shortly after it was just seen, and it is a basic effect in psychology.","children":[]},{"text":"Activation dynamics explain it because activation **does not rise and fall instantaneously**, and it may fall off more slowly than it rises.","children":["If the word is encountered again while activation is still high from the last exposure, its **baseline** is already higher than usual.","A node that starts from a higher baseline can be activated more easily, so the word is identified faster."]}],"m1-lecture04-p026":[{"text":"Two factors that influence the activity of a word are still unexplained. One is how similar the word is to other words, which produces **neighborhood effects**. The other is having just seen a similar or related word, which produces **associative, semantic or phonological priming**.","children":[]},{"text":"Both involve one word affecting a **different word**, while repetition involves the same word on two occasions.","children":["A set of separate nodes can explain changes in the activity of a single word but cannot explain an influence from another word, so something has to be **added to the network**.","What has to be added is connections between the nodes."]}],"m1-lecture04-p027":[{"text":"Understanding many phenomena in language requires assuming that the **nodes in a network are connected**.","children":[]},{"text":"In the network for lion, tiger and stripes, lion is linked to tiger and tiger is linked to stripes, so the nodes are no longer separate detectors.","children":["The printed word LION activates the lion node **directly**, and a connection can then carry some of that influence to tiger **indirectly**."]},{"text":"Connections matter because they change how one representation responds when another is active.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p028":[{"text":"One way to represent the associations from the norms is a network in which the **connection strength** between two nodes is determined by how strongly the words are associated.","children":["A semantic network of this kind links vehicle with car and bus, and it links fire engine both with vehicles and with concepts related to fire."]},{"text":"**Spreading activation** means that when \"lion\" is activated, some of that activation travels along a **weighted connection** to \"tiger\".","children":["Tiger is then already partly active, so it is easier to reach when the word tiger itself appears.","Connections of different strengths pass on different amounts of activity."]},{"text":"This is the spreading activation account of **semantic priming**, in which processing one word makes a related word easier to process.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p029":[],"m1-lecture04-p030":[{"text":"The word STRIPES sits in the center of a **strong noise mask** at the start of a perceptual identification sequence.","children":[]},{"text":"The contrast is low, so the word signal is hard to separate from the background and the word is barely visible.","children":["The stripes node can begin to receive some sensory support even while the evidence is **too weak for a confident identification**."]}],"m1-lecture04-p031":[{"text":"The **contrast of STRIPES increases**, so more of its letter pattern becomes available against the noise.","children":[]},{"text":"The input now gives stronger support for identifying the same word, although the letters are still faint.","children":["A change in clarity changes the **amount of evidence** for the target without changing which word the target is."]}],"m1-lecture04-p032":[{"text":"STRIPES becomes **more identifiable** as its contrast improves, and its letters are now visibly separate from the surrounding noise.","children":[]},{"text":"In the perceptual identification task the participant responds when the evidence is enough to recognize the word, so the quality of the stimulus decides **the point at which a response becomes possible**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p033":[{"text":"The clearer STRIPES gives **stronger word information** than the heavily masked versions, although the same kind of noise is still present.","children":[]},{"text":"A participant who recognizes the word can now make the identification response.","children":["The response reports **recognition of the target** and is not a judgment about whether stripes are associated with a particular animal."]}],"m1-lecture04-p034":[{"text":"At high contrast STRIPES stands out from the mask, and recognizing it **activates the stripes node** in the network account.","children":[]},{"text":"Identifying STRIPES also makes it a possible **prime**, which is a word seen first that can affect how another word is processed.","children":["Its effect on another word depends on the connections between their representations."]}],"m1-lecture04-p035":[{"text":"LION is the target in a **second perceptual identification sequence**, and it also begins with weak contrast against a noise mask, so its letters give little usable information.","children":[]},{"text":"The target and its visibility are separate from what was seen just before it.","children":["A priming account asks whether that preceding experience changes **how quickly LION is recognized**."]}],"m1-lecture04-p036":[{"text":"The contrast of LION increases, which **strengthens the perceptual evidence** for that word, although it is still only faintly visible in the center of the mask.","children":[]},{"text":"Recognition becomes more likely as the letter pattern separates from the noise.","children":["The same target produces a **changing internal response** because the quality of its sensory evidence keeps changing."]}],"m1-lecture04-p037":[{"text":"LION becomes clearer within the mask, and a response can occur once the evidence supports recognizing it.","children":["The **time of that response** is what the task measures."]},{"text":"A **priming comparison** asks whether recognition of the same target comes sooner after one preceding context than after another, so the visible target is what the two contexts have in common.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p038":[{"text":"LION is now legible while the noise mask is still present, so the input gives substantial evidence for the word and **activation of the lion node keeps increasing**.","children":[]},{"text":"Recognition starts from a state that has two sources, which are the **clarity of the target** and any **activation left behind by the context** that came before it.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p039":[{"text":"The clear LION completes the identification sequence.","children":[]},{"text":"The lion node can be activated in two ways, **directly by the word input** and **through connections with other nodes**.","children":["The difference between direct input and activity arriving through the network is what explains how seeing STRIPES first can prime LION."]}],"m1-lecture04-p040":[{"text":"Activity flows throughout the network, so activating \"stripes\" can **prime \"lion\"** even though these two words are **not directly associated**.","children":["Stripes is connected to tiger and tiger is connected to lion, so activation from stripes reaches lion by way of tiger."]},{"text":"This is called **mediated priming**, which is priming that passes through a middle word, with tiger as the intermediate node between the prime and the target.","children":["A model limited to direct associations has no route from stripes to lion, so it could not explain the effect.","The effect is for that reason **compelling evidence for spreading activation**."]}],"m1-lecture04-p041":[{"text":"The experiment with stripes and lion is very representative of psycholinguistics in two ways, which are **careful study design** and **small effects**.","children":[]},{"text":"The result is also **counterintuitive**, because \"stripes\" and \"lion\" are not obvious associates, yet \"stripes\" primes \"lion\" because activity travels through the connection each word has with tiger.","children":["The value of the experiment comes from the relationship it tests and not from the size of the difference.","An indirect route predicts an influence that a model with only direct associations could not explain, so a small effect can separate the two accounts."]}],"m1-lecture04-p042":[{"text":"**Bottom-up processing** carries information from visual features up to letters and then to words, and **top-down processing** lets an active word send activation back down to the letters that fit it.","children":[]},{"text":"In the Interactive Activation network, drawn after **McClelland and Rumelhart**, features are at the bottom, letters in the middle and words such as WORK, WORD, WEAR and WEAK at the top. When part of the last letter of WORK is covered, the partly visible features support several candidate letters.","children":["The display activates WORK at the word level, and WORK feeds activation back to K, so the output for **K rises strongly** over time while R and D stay low."]},{"text":"Top-down support of this kind is one way of modeling **pattern completion**, which Menn discusses at length.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p043":[{"text":"One extremely simple but powerful idea about how all of this gets into the brain is \"Neurons that fire together, wire together.\"","children":["This is **Hebbian learning**, in which units that are active together become more strongly connected, so learning changes the network that will process future input.","In the example of the bell and the food, both signals feed a pathway for salivation, so when they are active together the synapse from the bell onto that pathway is strengthened."]},{"text":"The reality is more complicated, but the neural learning mechanisms that are relatively well understood are **associative**.","children":[]},{"text":"Historically these mechanisms were considered **insufficient to learn anything important about language**. More recent arguments are about what role they might play and how **innate knowledge** might constrain them, especially given that LLMs can learn many structural features of language without much more than this.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p044":[{"text":"A **distributed representation** encodes an item as a pattern across many units, and it is how associative learning mechanisms can learn relatively sophisticated patterns.","children":[]},{"text":"**Generativity** is important for language, and for an associative model to **generalize** it has to be able to **represent similarity**.","children":["Similar items have overlapping patterns, so what is known about robins and canaries can generalize to new birds as they are learned about."]},{"text":"In the network, an item input and a relation input together produce attribute outputs.","children":["With the item canary and the relation can, the active attributes are grow, move, fly and sing."]}],"m1-lecture04-p045":[{"text":"Five **neurally inspired metaphors** give a way of talking about psycholinguistics. **Activation** changes as input is processed, and **spreading activation** lets one representation influence another.","children":["**Top-down and bottom-up processing** are the two directions of influence between levels.","**Associative learning**, such as Hebbian learning, changes connection strengths, and **distributed representations** give a basis for similarity and generalization."]},{"text":"**Priming experiments** measure how processing one item changes the processing of another, so people have used them to reason about what lexical networks might look like.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p046":[],"m1-lecture04-p047":[],"m1-lecture05-p001":[],"m1-lecture05-p002":[{"text":"**Phrenology** was an early attempt to relate brain structure to function by reading mental abilities from the shape of the skull.","children":["A simplified phrenological chart assigns regions of the head to language, memory, thinking, love and other categories."]},{"text":"Its reasoning rests on three premises, that **different parts of the brain** are responsible for different processes, that **aptitude** is related to differences in brain region size, and that **brain region size influences skull shape**. Careful measurements of people's skulls should then reveal relationships between skull shape and various brain functions.","children":[]},{"text":"No one believes in phrenology anymore, yet the premises are not equally wrong. The first premise, that brain parts differ in what they do, is separate from the claim about skulls and stays a serious idea, while the third fails because **skull shape is not a sensitive measure of brain shape**.","children":["The second premise would require a dependable link from an ability to the size of a region before any skull measurement could support a psychological claim."]}],"m1-lecture05-p003":[{"text":"**Phrenology** failed for two important reasons, one on the side of anatomy and one on the side of psychology.","children":[]},{"text":"The first problem is **inappropriate anatomical measurement**, because skull shape is not a sensitive measure of brain shape, so measuring the head cannot identify a brain mechanism.","children":[]},{"text":"The second problem is an **unconstrained definition of functions**, because broad social labels were treated as if they already named one mental function that could be assigned to a region.","children":["Studies of prisoners aimed to identify regions associated with \"criminality\", and studies of women with different numbers of children aimed to find the locus of \"philopropogenitiveness\".","The criticism is about the evidence linking those labels to regions of the head, so it leaves open the possibility that brain regions differ in what they contribute."]}],"m1-lecture05-p004":[{"text":"**Flourens** was an early and vociferous critic of phrenology who tested brain function with **experimental lesions**, which means damaging or removing brain tissue and then observing how behavior changes.","children":["He worked mainly with pigeons and rabbits, and he also wrote an anti-phrenology pamphlet."]},{"text":"His experiments established **distinct functions** for the cerebral cortex, the cerebellum and mid-brain structures, so he did find broad differences among large parts of the brain.","children":[]},{"text":"Within the cortex, however, he **failed to find any correspondence** between specific cortical regions and specific senses.","children":["He insisted instead that the **size of the lesion** was critical, because when one sense was lost, all were lost together, as when most of the cortex is removed and sight and hearing disappear together.","His lesion evidence argued against a precise map of sensory centers, so it supported a more general account of cortical function."]}],"m1-lecture05-p005":[{"text":"The neurologist **Pierre Paul Broca** presented the case of \"Tan\", a man who had lost the ability to speak after a brain injury decades earlier.","children":["He gave the presentation to the newly established Society of Anthropology, of which he was also the founding president.","This specific loss of language is called **aphasia**, and Tan was not the first observed case of a specific loss."]},{"text":"Tan **could only produce one jargon syllable**, yet he seemed otherwise to be \"in possession of his senses\" and seemed to **understand what was being said**.","children":[]},{"text":"Because speaking was severely impaired while understanding seemed preserved, the case suggests that **producing speech and understanding speech can be affected separately** by brain damage, so the loss was not a loss of intelligence or of all language ability.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p006":[{"text":"Tan's brain, kept on display at the Musee Dupuytren, has a **large lesion** that definitely includes the third convolution of the frontal lobe, also called the **inferior frontal gyrus**.","children":[]},{"text":"That link between the injury and the lost speech gives us **Broca's area**, a portion of the left frontal lobe that is important for speech.","children":["The lesion was extensive, so the injury was not a sharply isolated speech component."]},{"text":"**Hughlings Jackson** raised a criticism in the same period, \"to locate the damage which destroys speech and to locate speech are two different things\".","children":["Damage can interrupt a process through the region itself or through its connections, so the lost ability tells us the tissue matters for speech without telling us everything the intact system does."]}],"m1-lecture05-p007":[{"text":"German neurologists of the later nineteenth century, among them **Lichtheim** and **Wernicke**, began making more systematic observations of aphasia.","children":[]},{"text":"They ascribed specific losses of function to **disconnections between centers**, so a pathway between centers is part of the explanation along with the centers themselves.","children":["In Lichtheim's \"House\" model, **A** is the center for sound images, **M** is the center for movement images and **B** is the center for concepts, while lowercase a is the acoustic pathway and lowercase m is the motor pathway.","A loss of the ability to understand spoken words might be due to a disconnection between A and B, and a loss of verbatim repetition may be due to a disconnection between A and M. Each marked break in the model stands for a different form of aphasia."]},{"text":"Describing centers for specific functions is an ancestor of the later idea of **modularity**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p008":[{"text":"**Sigmund Freud**, in his early neurological research, critiqued the \"diagram-drawing\" school of aphasia research.","children":["He felt it was naive to expect the brain to be organized according to psychological categories like \"word forms\" or \"concepts\".","He proposed instead that language emerged from **fields** connecting perceptual and motor abilities to \"drives\"."]},{"text":"His position is a **holist critique**, and it marks a durable tension in cognitive neuroscience about whether brain area functions and cognitive functions can be directly related.","children":[]},{"text":"A psychological category can be useful for describing behavior, but it has to be connected to brain mechanisms **through evidence**, because the brain may not be organized according to our descriptions of cognitive phenomena.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p009":[{"text":"**Dronkers and colleagues** used **MRI** to assess the extent and location of the damage in Broca's landmark cases very precisely, including the brain of Leborgne, the patient called Tan.","children":[]},{"text":"The lesion is **very deep** and includes the **insula**, and there is also a lot of white matter damage to the **superior longitudinal fasciculus**, which connects the front and back of the cerebral cortex.","children":["The cases involved disrupted connections as well as cortical damage, which an inspection of the surface alone would not reveal."]},{"text":"Other work by this group suggests that the **insula, not Broca's area**, is strongly associated with **speech apraxia**, the condition Broca described in his patients.","children":["The historical case changes from a small surface speech center to a much more extensive pattern of injury, so the deficit cannot be assigned to the surface region alone."]}],"m1-lecture05-p010":[{"text":"We are still talking about Broca about a century and a half later even though both sides of his case were crude.","children":[]},{"text":"His **characterization of the behavior was crude**, because linguistics at the time was focused on historical issues, such as how languages were related to each other and how to reconstruct ancient languages.","children":["Experimental psychology had not really been invented yet, so observations of behavior were necessarily informal."]},{"text":"His **anatomical localization was also crude, and probably wrong**, because fine-grained anatomical descriptions of the cerebral cortex were still in their infancy.","children":["Brodmann's map, which divides the cortex into more detailed regions, was published only in the early twentieth century.","His lasting importance does not depend on his anatomical conclusion being a complete account of speech, because the case set up a question that later methods could study more carefully."]}],"m1-lecture05-p011":[{"text":"Other people, such as the **Daxes** and **Wernicke**, were reporting similar things at around the same time as Broca.","children":["Wernicke's data are, if anything, more interesting, because his patient was **fluent but produced meaningless gibberish**, which contrasts sharply with Tan's restricted speech.","Contrasting cases like these encourage separating the components of language instead of treating all abnormal speech as the same loss."]},{"text":"For a variety of reasons, though, Broca's presentation of Tan is seen as a **watershed moment** in neuropsychology generally.","children":["It turned the project of **relating specific functions to specific brain regions** into a respectable pursuit, which is why Broca is still discussed today."]}],"m1-lecture05-p012":[],"m1-lecture05-p013":[{"text":"**Cognitive neuropsychology** depends on cognitive psychology for its definitions of functions, and much of what was learned about structure-function relationships in the twentieth century came from experimental observations of individuals with brain damage.","children":[]},{"text":"Theory about cognitive processes supplies hypotheses about which operations might be separable, such as whether the ability to recognize the **abstract forms of words** can be **dissociated** from the ability to compute their meaning. A dissociation would mean that one of the two operations is impaired more than the other.","children":["Reaction time studies with normal subjects argue for different internal codes, because matching letters by **physical identity** is fastest, matching by **name identity** is slower, and matching by **rule identity**, such as both vowels or both consonants, is slowest.","The same ordering holds when pairs of words are classified as both animals or both plants."]},{"text":"**Quantitative measurements** allow statistical comparison, which is critical because deficits are **rarely complete**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p014":[{"text":"**Anatomical imaging** permits high-resolution location of brain injuries in living patients, so scientists no longer had to wait for patients to \"come to autopsy\" before determining the site of a lesion.","children":["A scan of HM's middle temporal lobes locates his damage, which was the result of surgery.","Such a scan identifies an injury and its extent, and it does not display activity during a language task."]},{"text":"The warning from **Hughlings Jackson** still applies, \"To locate the damage which destroys speech and to locate speech are two different things\".","children":["Studies of brain-injured patients tell us what **behavior looks like after a region is damaged**, so they can tell us only **indirectly** what the region might do when intact."]}],"m1-lecture05-p015":[{"text":"**Metabolic neuroimaging** allows us to study brains in action, and **Posner and colleagues** describe PET studies that used radio-labeled oxygen as a marker for activity.","children":[]},{"text":"Techniques like **PET** and **fMRI** are **metabolic**, so they do not record neural activity itself and instead give an index of oxygen consumption.","children":[]},{"text":"They are also **slow**, because metabolic processes unfold over seconds while important neural events can occur on the order of milliseconds or less.","children":["They have only **fair spatial resolution**, because their resolution is on the order of millimeters while important things are going on at the micron scale, although fMRI is better than PET.","A colored activation image is a measured metabolic signal, so it is not a direct view of thoughts or of single neurons firing."]}],"m1-lecture05-p016":[{"text":"The logic of cognitive psychology has to be applied to metabolic imaging data, because one cannot just say \"x part of the brain is active during y task\" when there is activity all over the brain all of the time.","children":[]},{"text":"A **control condition** is required for that reason, and **subtraction logic** takes the activity in the control condition away from the activity in the task condition, so what remains goes with whatever differs between the two.","children":["A scan taken during a motor task minus a scan taken at rest leaves a much smaller region than the widespread activity in either scan alone."]},{"text":"**Posner and colleagues** applied this logic to isolate identification of abstract word and letter forms from **semantic processing** of word meaning.","children":["Tasks can share visual input and response demands while differing in whether word meaning must be accessed, so the leftover activity goes with that one processing demand.","Monitoring words for dangerous animals minus passively viewing words is one such comparison."]}],"m1-lecture05-p017":[{"text":"**Posner and colleagues** found an area in the **left inferior prefrontal cortex** that was active in all three of their semantic processing conditions.","children":[]},{"text":"Each condition was a subtraction, so monitoring visual words for dangerous animals was compared with passively viewing words, and **generating uses** for words was compared with repeating them, once for visual words and once for auditory words.","children":["Generating a use requires retrieving meaning beyond what repeating the word requires, so the remaining activity goes with **semantic processing**.","The area was active whether the words were seen or heard, and each condition also activated the anterior cingulate gyrus.","The colors mark the relative strength of activation, from purple at the minimum to white at the maximum for that condition."]},{"text":"Interestingly, the region tied to semantic processing of words **corresponds roughly to Broca's area**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p018":[{"text":"Whether modern brain imaging is better than **phrenology** depends on the reasoning behind a localization claim and not on how attractive the image of an active region looks.","children":[]},{"text":"Phrenology had a poor anatomical measure and loosely defined functions, so a modern localization claim is better only when it has **valid measurements** and a **clear definition of the operation** being tested.","children":["An image of an active region does not supply those definitions by itself."]},{"text":"**Controlled task contrasts** give imaging studies a different kind of evidence from assigning broad traits to skull regions.","children":["The question to ask of any study is whether the contrast isolates the proposed operation and whether the measurement supports relating that operation to the observed region."]}],"m1-lecture05-p019":[{"text":"**Posner and colleagues** speak to the comparison with phrenology in their conclusions, where they claim localization of **component operations** and not of whole cognitive tasks.","children":[]},{"text":"The PET data provide strong support for localization of operations performed on **visual, phonological and semantic codes**, and the PET data on lexical access complement the lesion data.","children":["Studies of average blood flow could localize these operations, which suggests considerable homogeneity in the neural systems involved, at least among the right-handed subjects with good reading skills who were studied."]},{"text":"This form of localization differs from the idea that a cognitive task is performed by a particular brain area.","children":["Visual imagery, word reading and shifting visual attention are **not performed by any single brain area**, because each involves a large number of component computations that must be orchestrated."]}],"m1-lecture05-p020":[{"text":"Language has turned up at a number of key moments in debates about **localization of function**.","children":["Broca's presentation of Tan helped establish the neuropsychological approach of **lesion-behavior analysis**.","Some of the very first PET and fMRI studies used activation of \"Broca's Area\" in language tasks as evidence that cognitive functions could be localized."]},{"text":"Language suits these investigations because it offers **distinguishable abilities** that can be compared in behavior and in the brain.","children":["Restricted speech production, apparent comprehension, repetition and semantic judgments place different demands on processing, so an injury can affect one ability more than another.","Language tasks can also be broken into questions about forms, meanings and responses, which gives researchers a basis for choosing control conditions."]}],"m1-lecture05-p021":[],"m1-lecture06-p001":[],"m1-lecture06-p002":[{"text":"There is a very old tradition of describing natural languages in **abstract, formal terms**, similar to the terms used in **Artificial Intelligence**, so language is treated as symbols and rules.","children":["A grammar in this sense is an internal system that can characterize sentences a person has never encountered."]},{"text":"Experimental psychology in the fifties and sixties was **actively hostile to talking about this kind of internal state**, because behaviorists wanted to explain learning through observable stimuli, responses and consequences.","children":[]},{"text":"**Skinner** wrote Verbal Behavior to describe how language could be learned by **operant conditioning**, and **Chomsky** reviewed it so critically that the review is better remembered than the book.","children":["That review was an important moment in the **Cognitive Revolution**, which brought internal mental representations back into explanations of language."]}],"m1-lecture06-p003":[{"text":"The formal tradition comes from interest in **Artificial Intelligence**, which dates back to the beginning of the **digital computer**.","children":["**Alan Turing**, who developed decryption devices for the British Army during the Second World War, thought a lot about this question."]},{"text":"Computers were invented as **universal function approximators**, which means one machine could do many different kinds of calculations and logical operations instead of a single fixed task.","children":["No one had made a machine like that before, so people asked **how the computer differed from human intelligence, if at all**."]},{"text":"Because a computer runs procedures on symbols, it became a way to explore what language can do if language is also represented as symbols and procedures.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p004":[{"text":"The **Turing test** judges a machine by its conversation, and it starts from the **imitation game**.","children":["In the game, A and B are a man and a woman and C is an examiner whose job is to figure out the gender of A and B.","A is trying to fool C, while B is trying to help C guess the correct answer."]},{"text":"The test is that if either A or B can be replaced with a computer and **C does not notice**, the computer has demonstrated **intelligence**.","children":["The examiner never inspects the machine's program and only has its answers, so the machine passes by producing answers that fit the conversation."]}],"m1-lecture06-p005":[{"text":"Several features of the **Turing test** are worth knowing. The test runs entirely through **natural language**, and it involves **deception**, because a respondent has to handle an examiner who is trying to expose what it is.","children":[]},{"text":"Turing explicitly **excludes expert behaviors** such as calculating large sums or composing poetry, so the test is about ordinary conversation and not one special skill.","children":[]},{"text":"Turing concludes that the best way to get an intelligent machine would be to build something with the **cognitive capabilities of a child** and have it **learn** to be more like an adult.","children":["On this proposal, intelligence develops through experience instead of being supplied as a finished list of adult answers."]}],"m1-lecture06-p006":[{"text":"**Newell and Simon's General Problem Solver** is a later example of **GOFAI**, the approach that models intelligent behavior as operations on explicit symbols.","children":["Given an abstract characterization of a formal problem, the program **applies procedures to symbols to find a solution**."]},{"text":"It was very good at games and puzzles such as the **Tower of Hanoi** and **chess**, and also good at mathematics and logic such as **geometry proofs** and algebra word problems.","children":["These problems suit the program because their states and permitted moves can be stated explicitly.","In the Tower of Hanoi, each arrangement of disks is a state and each legal move leads to another state, so a solution is a path of legal moves that reaches the goal arrangement."]}],"m1-lecture06-p007":[{"text":"Early AI emphasized formal problems partly because of **technology**. Inputs to computers were still very limited, with punchcards and machine language, so there was no way to input a high-resolution image and ask the computer to find the face.","children":[]},{"text":"The other reason was **theoretical commitments**, because researchers held that intelligence is exemplified by this kind of **rational behavior**.","children":["A pigeon can be trained to guide a missile, but it does not know what it is doing.","There is a deep argument about whether AI programs know what they are doing either, but they at least **represent goal states and choose procedures** for achieving them."]}],"m1-lecture06-p008":[{"text":"**Chomsky's critique** makes several linked claims about what a speaker knows. A **grammar** that enumerates sentences and gives each one a structural description characterizes the ability to **tell sentences from nonsentences**, to **understand new sentences** and to notice certain **ambiguities**.","children":[]},{"text":"New sentences are not related to familiar ones by any simple formal, semantic or statistical similarity, so Chomsky calls talk of **generalization** pointless and empty.","children":["A new item is recognized as a sentence because it is **generated by the grammar** each person has internalized, and not because it matches a familiar item."]},{"text":"All normal children acquire comparable, very complex grammars with remarkable speed, so Chomsky suggests that humans are **specially designed** to do this, with a data-handling or hypothesis-formulating ability of unknown character.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p009":[{"text":"Chomsky characterizes linguistic knowledge as a **grammar**, which is **a set of abstract rules that produce grammatical sentences**.","children":["The grammar describes the ability to tell sentences from nonsentences, to understand new sentences and to notice ambiguities."]},{"text":"There are an **infinite number of grammatical sentences**, so it is remarkable that a person can make a **grammaticality judgment** for any new sentence.","children":["A grammaticality judgment is a person's sense of whether a sentence is well formed or ill formed."]},{"text":"In this approach, **people's intuitions about whether sentences are grammatical are the key data** for the study of language.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p010":[{"text":"Chomsky argues that **grammatical sentences do not have any obvious similarities to one another**, whether in form, meaning, statistics or grammatical frame.","children":["A new sentence is accepted because it is generated by the grammar a person has internalized, and it is understood in part because the person can determine how the grammar derives it."]},{"text":"**Stimulus generalization** is the behaviorist concept in which a trained response extends to stimuli that resemble the trained one.","children":["Because it depends on resemblance and grammatical sentences do not resemble one another in any simple way, stimulus generalization **cannot deal with the infinite number of sentences** a grammar can generate."]}],"m1-lecture06-p011":[{"text":"In behaviorism, **stimulus generalization** happens when a trained response spreads to similar stimuli. A pigeon trained to peck for a **green** light also pecks for similar colors, with the most pecks at green and fewer pecks as the color gets less like green.","children":["Another pigeon does a **magnitude discrimination task**, where the stimuli differ in numerical magnitude instead of color."]},{"text":"The cases in which operant conditioning generalizes depend on **relatively concrete similarity**, such as color or number.","children":["It is **not clear how this extends to abstract concepts**, so a conditioning account of language would have to say what kind of similarity links grammatical sentences."]}],"m1-lecture06-p012":[{"text":"**Stimulus control** is the idea in Skinner's account that some aspect of the environment controls a spoken response.","children":["A typical example for Skinner is a person responding to a piece of music with an utterance, and in another example a proper noun is held to be a response."]},{"text":"Chomsky says such examples **merely add to the general mystification**. A piece of music has many properties and a speaker can respond to it in different ways, so the account must say which property controls the utterance.","children":["The explanation only works if the stimulus can be identified **independently of the utterance**, because otherwise the response itself decides what counts as its cause."]}],"m1-lecture06-p013":[{"text":"Chomsky writes that verbal behavior **cannot be predicted from the stimuli** in the speaker's environment, because nobody knows what the current stimuli are until the speaker responds.","children":["Naming the stimulus only after hearing the response makes the prediction **circular**."]},{"text":"Read this way, Skinner does not only have a bad theory of language, because he is **posing the wrong question**.","children":["Why people say what they do, when they do, depends on a whole matrix of social interactions, cognitive processes and previous experiences, and much of that has nothing to do with language."]},{"text":"The **tractable problem** is to study the **structure of what people say** and from there derive the **set of rules** that produces this structure.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p014":[{"text":"Chomsky points out that neither \"colorless green ideas sleep furiously\" nor \"furiously sleep ideas green colorless\", nor any of their parts, has ever occurred in the past linguistic experience of an English speaker.","children":["The first is **grammatical** and the second is **not**, even though both use the same words, so the judgment cannot come from having met the sentence before.","The first is also hard to interpret literally, so **grammatical structure and meaningfulness come apart**."]},{"text":"The warning \"Car! Getting towed! Yours!\" is **ungrammatical**, although the stimulus that likely controlled it is perfectly clear.","children":["A clear controlling stimulus does not make an utterance grammatical, so being understood in a situation and being grammatical are separate questions."]}],"m1-lecture06-p015":[{"text":"Chomsky is trying to describe language at the **computational level of analysis**. The term comes from **David Marr**, a visual neuroscientist who described cognitive systems as having three **levels of analysis**.","children":[]},{"text":"The **computational** level says what problem the system solves.","children":["The **algorithmic** level says which representations and procedures are used to solve it.","The **implementational** level says how those procedures are physically carried out."]},{"text":"The three levels answer different questions about the same ability, so a claim can be informative at one level without answering the questions at the other levels.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p016":[{"text":"A **cash register** is the worked case for the three levels. At the **computational level**, it takes the prices of items and determines the amount due for a purchase, and it takes the payment amount and computes the change.","children":[]},{"text":"At the **algorithmic level**, it uses the operations of **addition** for the prices and **subtraction** for the change.","children":[]},{"text":"The **implementational level does not matter** for these functions, because a credit card reader or a computer program does the same job and so counts as a cash register too.","children":["The physical devices differ, but the relation between the prices, the payment and the required answer stays the same."]}],"m1-lecture06-p017":[{"text":"Marr called problems that break down cleanly into these levels **Type I problems**.","children":[]},{"text":"Viewing **language as a Type I problem** means a researcher can work at the **computational level**, which is **Universal Grammar**, and at the **algorithmic level**, which is the specific processes and how they are applied in different languages.","children":["The researcher can do this **without worrying about the implementational level**, which covers errors, memory limitations and associative learning."]},{"text":"Chomsky's critique came about a decade before Marr's theoretical work, so Marr's terms describe Chomsky's approach and are not where it came from.","children":["Both were influential in artificial intelligence research and theoretical work in the GOFAI tradition."]}],"m1-lecture06-p018":[{"text":"Marr says Chomsky's notion of a **competence theory** for English syntax is exactly what he means by a **computational theory**. Both are little concerned with the details of the algorithms that must be run, because a person has to know what the algorithms are supposed to do before devising them.","children":["When a problem decomposes in this way, Marr says it has a **Type I theory**."]},{"text":"Marr adds that there is no reason why every problem of biological information processing should have a Type I theory.","children":["A problem can be solved by the simultaneous action of a considerable number of processes **whose interaction is its own simplest description**, and Marr calls that situation a **Type II theory**."]}],"m1-lecture06-p019":[{"text":"Marr applies the two types to the **grammar of natural language**. He says the purpose of human language is presumably to transform a data structure that is not one-dimensional into **one-dimensional form** for transmission as a **sequential utterance**.","children":["The listener then retranslates the utterance into a rough copy of the original."]},{"text":"Seen this way, there may be **no Type I theory of English syntax** of the kind transformational grammar attempts to define.","children":["The constraints of syntax may be wired-in conventions about useful ways of carrying out this operation, and not deep principles about the nature of intelligence.","An abstract theory of syntax may then be an illusion that only approximates what the processes really do, so the grammar of natural language **may have a Type II theory**."]}],"m1-lecture06-p020":[{"text":"If grammar has a Type II theory, details about **how language is implemented in the brain** might be **unavoidable in a theory of language**.","children":["Facts about learning, memory, perception and action would then be part of the explanation of linguistic structure, because the processes that do the work would shape what the system can represent."]},{"text":"A whole **alternative approach** to language research is based on just this hypothesis.","children":["That approach starts with the properties of the learner and the processing system and asks how they produce the regularities seen in language use, instead of starting with an abstract grammar."]}],"m1-lecture06-p021":[{"text":"**Emergentism**, **statistical learning** and **parallel distributed processing** name the alternative view, and its strong commitments are at the **implementational level**.","children":[]},{"text":"It assumes **distributed, non-hierarchical representations**, which are like patterns of activity over groups of neurons.","children":["A representation is spread over many units instead of sitting in a single symbol."]},{"text":"It assumes **gradual learning**, which is like adjusting the strength of synaptic connections.","children":["It also focuses on **microstructure** and makes a more **graded division between the levels of analysis**, so properties of the learning mechanism are expected to shape the apparent rules in behavior."]}],"m1-lecture06-p022":[{"text":"**Elman** built **simple recurrent networks** to capture different aspects of language performance in this framework.","children":["All the model does is try to **predict the next item** it is going to see, and it gradually learns to do this with experience."]},{"text":"The current input activates **hidden units**, and the hidden units produce the output prediction.","children":["A copy of the hidden activity is carried into **context units**, so the context units supply information about the earlier items when the next input arrives.","Because the same item can follow different earlier sequences, a good prediction depends on both the current item and that context."]}],"m1-lecture06-p023":[{"text":"A **letter-prediction model** built on these principles can **find words** in text that has no spaces.","children":["The unbroken string \"Manyyearsagoaboyandgirllivedbytheseatheyplayedhappily\" becomes \"many years ago a boy and girl lived by the sea they played happily\"."]},{"text":"The model is only predicting the next letter, and its **prediction error goes down within words**.","children":["Inside a word the earlier letters limit what can come next, so prediction gets easier.","At the start of a new word many more letters are possible, so the error often rises again, and those rises line up with the **word boundaries**."]}],"m1-lecture06-p024":[{"text":"When a similar model is trained on **simple sentences**, it starts to identify **grammatical classes** and even some **semantic information**.","children":["This happens as a **side effect** of learning to predict the sequences, because the model was never asked to classify words."]},{"text":"Elman examined the hidden activity for each word, and words that occur in similar contexts ended up with similar activity.","children":["**Nouns** separate from **verbs**, and the nouns divide into animate and inanimate groups with smaller clusters for humans, animals, food and breakable things.","The verbs divide by their patterns of direct-object use."]}],"m1-lecture06-p025":[{"text":"Many engineering solutions in **Natural Language Processing** work this way, by statistically analyzing massive **corpora**, which are collections of texts and transcriptions of spoken language.","children":[]},{"text":"**Peter Norvig** agrees with Chomsky that neither sentence had appeared in the published literature before Chomsky wrote, but he finds that **every two-word part had already occurred**, such as \"colorless green\", \"green ideas\" and \"Ideas sleep\".","children":[]},{"text":"Norvig reports that **Pereira** used a statistically-trained **finite-state model**, augmented with word categories and trained by expectation maximization on newspaper text.","children":["The model computes that \"colorless green ideas sleep furiously\" is **two hundred thousand times more probable** than the reversed order, so a statistical model **can distinguish the two sentences**."]}],"m1-lecture06-p026":[],"m1-lecture06-p027":[{"text":"Psycholinguistics has two important traditions, and the first is the **GOFAI, modular or Chomskyan** tradition. It assumes that linguistic knowledge is essentially **abstract and symbolic** and that linguistic representations are **different in kind** from other kinds of knowledge.","children":["All of this has to interface with messy perceptual, motor, memory and other cognitive capacities, so the psycholinguist's job is to **study this interface**."]},{"text":"The **emergentist or functionalist** tradition assumes that symbol-like and rule-like behavior are **emergent properties of a statistical learning process**.","children":["It also assumes that perceptual, motor and cognitive processes **shape linguistic representations**, so the psycholinguist's job is to study **language use** and relate it to more general psychological phenomena."]}],"m1-lecture06-p028":[],"m1-lecture06-p029":[],"m1-lecture06-p030":[{"text":"**Emergentist and statistical learning approaches** start from the messiness seen in the \"i before e\" rule, which is found all over the place in every language.","children":[]},{"text":"**Phonetic categories** are probabilistically defined over multiple dimensions, and **words are ambiguous** in every way imaginable.","children":["**Morphological paradigms** such as adding -ed for the past tense in English have exceptions like RUN-RAN and SLEEP-SLEPT.","**Syntactic rules** are open to dialect and individual variation, innovation and change over time."]},{"text":"Because a strong pattern and its exceptions exist side by side at every level, these approaches let experience shape graded expectations instead of assuming exceptionless rules.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p031":[{"text":"**Moravec's paradox** is a problem for the GOFAI approach. Proving theorems and solving geometry problems is **comparatively easy for computers**, but a supposedly simple task like recognizing a face or crossing a room without bumping into anything is **extremely difficult**.","children":["The opposite is true for humans."]},{"text":"So characterizing language in formal terms, the way a computer program would be written, **may be a false trail**.","children":["For language research, the paradox is a reason to take perceptual, motor and other processing systems seriously instead of treating them as simple attachments to a symbolic grammar."]}],"m1-lecture06-p032":[{"text":"Another problem for the formal approach is that **fixed categories and rules are often fuzzy in practice**, which has been recognized for a long time.","children":[]},{"text":"**Wittgenstein** asks what is common to everything called a **game**, including board games, card games, ball games and Olympic games.","children":["He says not to assume there must be something common, but to look and see whether there is.","Looking does not turn up one thing common to all games, and what appears is **similarities and relationships**, a whole series of them."]},{"text":"Moving from board games to card games, many correspondences with the first group remain while other features change, so the category holds together without one defining feature.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p033":[{"text":"**Many phenomena in language are probabilistic**, and Wittgenstein's games are a category of that kind. From card games to ball games, much that is common is kept but much is lost.","children":[]},{"text":"No single feature runs through all games.","children":["Amusement differs between chess and noughts and crosses, patience has no competition between players, and winning and losing disappears when a child throws a ball at the wall and catches it again.","Skill in chess differs from skill in tennis, and ring-a-ring-a-roses keeps the amusement while many other features disappear."]},{"text":"The result is **a complicated network of similarities overlapping and criss-crossing**, sometimes overall similarities and sometimes similarities of detail.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p034":[{"text":"**Family resemblances**, **similarity-based generalization** and **hunches** were turning up across psychology.","children":[]},{"text":"**Roger Shepard** worked on perception, **Eleanor Rosch** on categorization, and **Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky** on decision-making.","children":["They and many others were producing results that made a lot of cognition seem **less amenable to formal, rational interpretation**."]},{"text":"The named researchers worked in different areas and did not share one experiment, so several lines of psychology were pointing the same way.","children":["That makes it reasonable to ask whether language also reflects similarity-based generalization and the properties of actual processing."]}],"m1-lecture07-p001":[],"m1-lecture07-p002":[{"text":"People make **speech errors** because many words and sounds are active while an utterance is planned, so sometimes the wrong one is selected.","children":[]},{"text":"Errors interest psycholinguists because their form is **evidence about planning**, since an error can split parts of a word, move a sound to another position or swap in a related word.","children":["Comparing the intended and produced forms reveals **which units** the production system represents and how it organizes them."]},{"text":"**Fromkin** titled the article “Speech errors as linguistic evidence,” which was gutsy because linguists often study **competence** apart from the slips of **performance**, so errors could simply be set aside.","children":["The title claims instead that the structure of an error tells us how language itself is organized."]}],"m1-lecture07-p003":[{"text":"**Spreading activation** models explain speech errors as competition, because activating one word also activates related items that can be selected by mistake.","children":["**Noise** here means not only acoustic noise but also fluctuations and interference in the activation of different parts of the system."]},{"text":"**Frequency** matters because common words are easier to activate than rare ones, and this seems to affect many levels of speech production.","children":[]},{"text":"**Other sources of activation** add competitors, since what you just said and what you plan to say stay partly active while you talk, and so can things you are trying not to say.","children":["**Similarity** lets activation spread more easily between related items, whether the relation is semantic, syntactic or phonological."]}],"m1-lecture07-p004":[{"text":"A **tip-of-the-tongue (TOT) state** is partial activation of a word’s meaning and sound form that falls short of the **threshold** for producing it, so you know the word but cannot say it.","children":[]},{"text":"**Less common words** are more susceptible to TOT states, because by the frequency principle their forms are harder to activate.","children":["In the study reviewed by **Lise Abrams**, people in a TOT state for “contraband,” defined as goods traded illegally, read a list of words before trying again."]},{"text":"**Priming** helps because a word that shares sounds with the target adds activation to the missing form.","children":["Words sharing the **first syllable**, such as “contact,” “connotation” and “conscious,” resolved **about half** of TOT states, compared with about a quarter after unrelated words.","Middle-syllable overlap helped less, and final-syllable overlap was close to the unrelated words."]}],"m1-lecture07-p005":[{"text":"**Slips of the tongue** differ in the direction a sound moves, and all of these examples come from **Fromkin**.","children":["**William Archibald Spooner** supposedly once said “Mardon me padam, this pie is occupewed,” a slip full of moved sounds."]},{"text":"In **anticipation**, a later sound appears too early, as when “week long race” becomes “reek long race.”","children":["In **perseveration**, an earlier sound carries into a later position, as when “John gave the boy” becomes “John gave the goy.”"]},{"text":"In **metathesis**, also called an exchange, two sounds trade places, as when “keep a tape” becomes “teep a cape.”","children":["Classifying a slip depends on comparing the intended and produced forms, not on whether the result makes sense."]}],"m1-lecture07-p006":[{"text":"**Other sources of activation** are most obviously at work in these slips, because each one borrows a sound from elsewhere in the same utterance.","children":["In **anticipation**, material planned for a later position is already active, so the r in “reek long race” arrives early.","In **perseveration**, material just spoken stays active, so the g in “John gave the goy” lingers."]},{"text":"In **metathesis**, two elements active in the same plan interfere with each other and trade positions.","children":[]},{"text":"**Similarity** and **frequency** also play a part because they affect which candidates compete well, and **noise** in activation affects which one is finally selected.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p007":[{"text":"**Mondegreens** are **slips of the ear**, in which a listener assigns the speech sounds to different words than the speaker intended.","children":["The term comes from mishearing “laid him on the green” as “Lady Mondegreen,” and “Hold me closer, tiny dancer” is misheard as “Hold me closer, Tony Danza.”","**Andrew Nevins** is analyzing crowdsourced mishearings collected on the kissthisguy website."]},{"text":"Mondegreens happen because speech is continuous, so the listener has to infer the **word boundaries**.","children":["“The good candy came anyways” and “The good can decay many ways” contain nearly the same sounds, so **context** is what tells them apart."]},{"text":"Songs make this harder because **prosody**, the rhythm and melody of speech, is less helpful when words are sung.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p008":[{"text":"**Malapropisms** of this kind are **perceptual errors** more than speech errors, because the speaker says exactly what they intend to say.","children":[]},{"text":"The speaker seems to have heard an unfamiliar word like “congenital” and decided that a more familiar word, “congenial,” must have been intended, so they later say “a congenial heart defect.”","children":["“Congenital” means present from birth and “congenial” means pleasing, so only their sound links them."]},{"text":"This is **top-down processing** at work, because knowledge of familiar words shapes how the heard sound is interpreted.","children":["The mistake happened during perception, so the odd phrase sounds like a slip but arises in a different way."]}],"m1-lecture07-p009":[{"text":"When **Fromkin** wrote this paper, researchers argued about whether the **phoneme**, or phone, a single speech sound, was a unit in speech planning.","children":[]},{"text":"If whole **consonant clusters** always moved together, “fish grotto” would have become “gish frotto” and “fresh clear water” would have become “clesh freer water.”","children":[]},{"text":"Instead, Fromkin presents evidence that clusters get **broken up all the time** in production, so single segments must be planning units.","children":["In “fish grotto” becoming “frish gotto,” only the r leaves the gr cluster and joins the f.","In “fresh clear water” becoming “flesh queer water,” the r and l trade places while the f and k stay where they were."]}],"m1-lecture07-p010":[{"text":"**Fromkin** drew an early version of the **production model** that **Menn** describes, which traces how an intended meaning becomes speech.","children":[]},{"text":"The process starts with the **meaning to be conveyed**, builds **syntactic and semantic structure**, and adds **intonation** and information from the **lexicon** partway through.","children":["This produces strings of segments divided into syllables with their features, which **morphophonemic rules** and **phonetic rules** then specify fully."]},{"text":"Finally, **motor commands to the muscles** produce the **utterance**.","children":["Because the model has separate stages, an error can disturb one stage while the others stay intact, so different kinds of errors are evidence about different stages."]}],"m1-lecture07-p011":[{"text":"The basic model of **speech production** has five levels, the **message**, **functional**, **positional**, **phonological encoding** and **speech gesture** levels.","children":[]},{"text":"The **message level** is what the speaker intends to convey, and the **functional level** selects **lemmas** and links them to semantic roles.","children":["Later levels arrange the clause, build its sound form and prepare the movements that say it."]},{"text":"Following **Menn**, the focus for now is the message and functional levels, although the earlier sound slips were already **phonological encoding** errors.","children":["Separating the levels matters because different errors have different structures, so each kind of error points to a different level."]}],"m1-lecture07-p012":[{"text":"The **message level** is where the speaker decides what information to communicate, and the same event can become quite different messages.","children":[]},{"text":"You might tone down “I won a bunch of money on the game Sunday” for someone rooting for the losing team, because their feelings affect how directly you emphasize the win.","children":["For someone not interested in football you might explain why the game matters, and in a tweet you would choose only a few details."]},{"text":"The message level is **mysterious and hard to investigate experimentally**, because giving speakers the same event does not ensure they intend the same content.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p013":[{"text":"Some speech production studies try to control the message level with an **elicitation paradigm**, a method in which every speaker describes the same material, such as the picture book “Frog, where are you?”","children":["The book shows a boy, a dog and frogs, so all speakers share the same characters and scenes."]},{"text":"It is **still hard to define the intended message** for a scene like this, because speakers can focus on different characters, actions or relations.","children":["How a speaker builds that message may also depend on **age**, **culture** and **audience**, so the method controls what people see more than what they mean to say."]}],"m1-lecture07-p014":[{"text":"Speakers cannot simply select finished “words” at the **functional level**, and a verb exchange error explains why.","children":["“Where’d I put it when I brought it in” came out as “Where’d I bring it when I put it in.”"]},{"text":"The verbs switched places, but the moved verb appeared as “bring” rather than “brought,” so the tense must have been applied after the exchange.","children":["If the word “brought” itself had been selected, it would have moved as “brought.”"]},{"text":"So the speaker selected the **lemma** BRING, a word’s identity before its final grammatical form is fixed, and it was expressed as present tense in its new position.","children":["The term **lemma** is borrowed from mathematics, where it refers to a proposition in an argument."]}],"m1-lecture07-p015":[{"text":"People often say the **opposite** of what they mean, and these slips are **errors in lemma selection**, where the wrong lemma wins the competition.","children":["One speaker said “I really like to” before correcting it to “hate to get up in the morning.”","Others said “bottom” for “top” of the stack of books, “hot” for “cold,” and “the oral” for “written part of the exam.”"]},{"text":"The wrong word comes from the same domain as the intended one, so the sentence stays grammatical while its meaning is reversed.","children":[]},{"text":"This happens because an antonym is related in meaning, strongly associated and able to fill the same roles, which makes it an unusually strong competitor.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p016":[{"text":"Antonyms compete so well because they are **similar at a number of levels**, so it is easy for activation to spread from a **lemma** to its antonym.","children":["They are **semantically related** and **strongly associated**, which makes them a perfect storm in the spreading activation framework."]},{"text":"Antonyms can also take on the **same semantic roles** and belong to the **same word class**, so they can fill the same syntactic roles.","children":["Because of this, the rest of the speech production system can handle the wrong word, as when “hot” replaces “cold” in an otherwise normal sentence."]}],"m1-lecture07-p017":[{"text":"A **blend** combines parts of two competing words into a single form.","children":["**Lewis Carroll**, in his preface to The Hunting of the Snark, explained his **portmanteau** words this way, saying that someone who means to say both “fuming” and “furious” with a perfectly balanced mind will say “frumious.”"]},{"text":"**Fromkin** quoted Carroll because he claimed such blends are possible in natural speech and proposed a hypothesis about how they occur.","children":["Fromkin added that such complete blends are seldom found in normal speech, so a blend of “furious” and “fuming” is more apt to come out as [fjumijəs] or [fjuriŋ].","This happens particularly because the **first syllables are identical**, so the words join where their sounds overlap."]}],"m1-lecture07-p018":[{"text":"Spontaneous **blends** show speakers competing between **alternative words** with similar meanings.","children":["Examples blend “mainly” with “mostly,” “switch” with “changed” to give “swindged,” “bother” with “trouble,” and “player” with “athlete.”"]},{"text":"**Fromkin** proposed that a speaker matches **semantic features** while selecting words, and when there are several alternatives, both go into a **buffer** with their sound specifications.","children":["Either a selection happens there or the words are blended, so these errors seem to support Carroll’s assumptions."]},{"text":"There are almost always a number of alternative possibilities in speech production, so blends reveal competition that is an ordinary part of speaking.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p019":[{"text":"**Semantic roles**, also called functional roles, describe how each participant relates to an event.","children":["In “The plums in the icebox were eaten by me,” the order is Undergoer, Location, Verb, Agent.","In “I have eaten the plums in the icebox,” the order is Agent, Verb, Undergoer, Location."]},{"text":"With this simple event structure, lemmas can be slotted into roles, so the verb is TO EAT, the **Agent** or doer is ME, and PLUM is the **Undergoer** that is affected.","children":[]},{"text":"Arranging the roles in different orders has **syntactic consequences**, such as whether ME is said as “I” or “me,” and that is decided at the **Positional Level**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p020":[{"text":"By the end of the **functional level**, the speaker has decided on a **message**, selected appropriate **lemmas**, and associated those lemmas with **semantic roles**.","children":[]},{"text":"That is quite a bit, yet the speaker is **nowhere near putting a clause together**, much less saying it out loud.","children":["For an eating event the plan has EAT, the eater and the thing eaten, but not whether the sentence is active or passive, how pronouns are said, or any sounds."]},{"text":"The remaining work of ordering and pronouncing the clause comes at later levels, beginning with the **Positional Level**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p021":[],"m1-lecture07-p022":[],"m1-lecture07-p023":[{"text":"**Speech errors very often go undetected**, because **top-down effects** in speech perception let listeners recover the intended message despite odd wording.","children":["The process is similar to what must have happened for many **malapropisms** and **mondegreens**."]},{"text":"Some “errors” are **calculated for stylistic reasons**, as when **Paul Krugman** wrote “the upper hand is on the other foot,” quoting the film Hot Shots Part Deux.","children":["The line mixes two familiar idioms, “the upper hand” and the shoe being on the other foot."]},{"text":"A deliberate error can be **more memorable** than its correct counterpart because the mismatch draws attention to the wording itself, while an ordinary slip passes unnoticed as the listener focuses on the expected message.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p001":[],"m1-lecture08-p002":[{"text":"The bottom three levels of the production model are the **positional level**, the **phonological encoding level** and the **speech gestural level**, which carry a planned message down to articulated speech.","children":["The positional level builds the grammatical structure, phonological encoding plans the string of phonemes, and the gestural level turns that plan into movements."]},{"text":"Speech errors matter here because one error can keep one kind of organization while breaking another, so errors give evidence that **abstract entities** like **phonemes**, **lemmas** (the words chosen at the functional level) and **noun phrases** are useful units.","children":["Each collected error can then be traced to the level where it most likely happened."]},{"text":"Purely **observational research** on errors also has real weaknesses, and those weaknesses are the reason to study speech production with **experimental methods** next.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p003":[{"text":"By the end of the **functional level**, the sentence \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in?\" already has its two verbs, PUT and BRING, and its **semantic roles**, the jobs each participant plays.","children":["I/ME is the **Agent** (the one doing the action) of both verbs, and IT is the **Undergoer** (the thing acted on) of both verbs."]},{"text":"The speaker also has to work out the **temporal relationship** between the verbs, meaning how the putting and the bringing line up in time, and that the sentence is a question.","children":[]},{"text":"Expressing all of those facts **syntactically** is the job of the **positional level**, so it turns verbs and roles into clauses with grammatical positions.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p004":[{"text":"Leaving aside the work of forming the question and choosing tenses, the positional level produces a frame like \"Where'd I [verb, root form] it when I [verb, past] it in?\"","children":["Even the first clause takes a lot of syntactic work, because the question needs both **Wh- movement** (moving the question word to the front) and an **auxiliary verb**."]},{"text":"The structure of the first clause requires the **base form** of the lemma in English, while the next clause requires the **past form**.","children":[]},{"text":"So the positional level holds a **syntactic structure with slots** for every lemma activated at the functional level, plus the other particles the grammar requires.","children":["The error \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in\" can arise while those slots are being **filled with lemmas**, so BRING and PUT end up in each other's slots."]}],"m1-lecture08-p005":[{"text":"In \"a language needer learns\", the speaker meant to say \"a language learner needs\", so this is another error worth comparing with the BRING and PUT sentence.","children":[]},{"text":"It is similar because **two lemmas**, LEARN and NEED, **got switched** while being fed into the positional level, and the positional structure stayed almost unchanged.","children":[]},{"text":"It is different because the noun is really a **verb plus an affix**, so the ending -er stays in the noun slot and the ending -s stays in the verb slot while the two roots trade places.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p006":[{"text":"In \"a language needer learns\", two lemmas got switched as they were fed into the positional level, while the **positional level itself stayed the same**.","children":[]},{"text":"The difference from the earlier sentence is in the noun, which is actually a **verb plus an affix**.","children":["This is **derivational morphology** (adding a piece that builds a new kind of word), so adding -er to the verb learn makes the noun learner.","The verb carries the third-person singular ending -s, which is **inflectional morphology** because it marks a grammatical requirement on the verb."]},{"text":"Because both endings stay in their positions while the roots move, the error is **not a swap of two whole words**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p007":[{"text":"In \"He did that easy enoughly\", the speaker meant \"easily enough\", so the ending -ly, which builds an adverb, landed on enough instead of easy.","children":[]},{"text":"This is similar to \"a language needer learns\" because both errors leave a **stranded affix**, an ending that stays in its position while the wrong root fills that position.","children":[]},{"text":"It is different because something also went wrong **while setting up the positional level**, not only while its slots were being filled.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p008":[{"text":"Both \"He did that easy enoughly\" and \"a language needer learns\" have a **stranded affix**.","children":["Here the affix is -ly, which you can add to turn an **adjective into an adverb**."]},{"text":"The difference is that something also happened **when setting up the positional level**, so the frame itself was arranged differently.","children":["The intended phrase puts the adverb position, built from an adjective plus -ly, before the adjective, but the starred structure puts the adverb position after the adjective.","Because enough fills that adverb position, the speaker produces enoughly and leaves easy without its ending."]}],"m1-lecture08-p009":[{"text":"These errors are evidence that **derivational morphology** is a real process, because **affixes** seem able to **float around** at the positional level.","children":[]},{"text":"An alternative view says that **derived forms** are just other stored words related to their roots by rule, as in needy, need and needless or unlearn, learn and learner.","children":["That lexicon would need no separate representation for affixes, and nobody would have to remember quirky rules for making common words."]},{"text":"But errors create **non-words** like \"needer\" and \"enoughly\" **on the fly**, so the errors suggest that lemmas and affixes are **represented separately**.","children":["They also suggest that affixes have a **slot** at the positional level and that derived words can be made by a **grammatical process**."]}],"m1-lecture08-p010":[{"text":"Adding an affix also changes the **sound form** of the word, so easy /’i.zi/ becomes easily /’i.zIl.i/ and enough /i.’nəf/ becomes enoughly /i.’nəf.li/.","children":[]},{"text":"Derivational morphology can involve **complex phonological processes**, as in certificate /sər.ˈtI.fɨ.kɨt/, certification /ˌsər.tɨ.fɨ.ˈkeI.ʃən/ and recertification /ˈri.sər.tɨ.fɨ.ˌkeI.ʃən/, where **vowels change** and **stress moves**.","children":[]},{"text":"So when the positional level specifies a process like \"add +er\" or \"make this adjective into an adverb\", it **still leaves work to do** for the levels that plan the sounds.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p011":[{"text":"**Phonological encoding** takes the specification of lemmas, affixes and syntactic particles and turns it into a **string of phonemes** to produce.","children":[]},{"text":"For \"easy enoughly\" this level seems to have **worked fine**, because the speaker did not say \"easy /inəvɪli/\" or make any other error in the phoneme sequence.","children":[]},{"text":"Errors at this level include \"fish grotto\" becoming \"gish frotto\", \"Chomsky and Halle\" becoming \"Chomsky and Challe\", and \"with this ring I do wed\" becoming \"with this wing I do red\".","children":["In each case the problem is specifically with the **ordering of phonemes**.","**Menn** calls these errors **neat**, because they follow the **phonotactic patterns** of English (the allowed sound sequences) and, as far as anyone knows, are clearly produced."]}],"m1-lecture08-p012":[{"text":"At the **speech gestural level**, the sequence of phonemes planned during phonological encoding becomes a series of **gestures** actually carried out by the **motor system**.","children":[]},{"text":"Unlike phonological encoding errors, gestural errors are **not necessarily neat**, because they lead to **unclear or imprecise speech**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Many errors are ambiguous**, so \"ad hoc\" becoming \"odd hack\", with /æd hɑk/ coming out as /ɑd hæk/, could be **metathesis** (two sounds trading places) at the phonological encoding level.","children":["It could also be a **miscuing of tongue position** at the gestural level, and either route changes the vowels a listener hears."]}],"m1-lecture08-p013":[],"m1-lecture08-p014":[{"text":"Saying \"buyed in\" for \"bought in\" is a **morphological regularization**, because the regular past ending is added to a verb whose past form is irregular.","children":[]},{"text":"\"arising\" coming out as \"arriving\" changes a consonant inside a very similar word form.","children":[]},{"text":"\"It's pretty [kloʊz] to here\" for \"it's pretty close to here\" ends the word with a voiced consonant, and \"throfly\" for \"Thoroughly\" changes several parts of the sound sequence, so both are **sound-level errors**.","children":["Calling all four just wrong words would hide the **different levels** where they happened."]}],"m1-lecture08-p015":[{"text":"\"Wash my teeth and brush my face\" **exchanges the verbs** while the objects stay in place, so the clause structure survives.","children":[]},{"text":"\"following vowel\" becoming \"vollowing fowel\" **exchanges initial consonants**, and \"seat heaters\" becoming \"heat seaters\" moves sounds while the plural ending stays on seaters.","children":["These errors separate the movement of **whole roots** from the movement of **single sounds**."]},{"text":"\"Lana del Rey\" becoming \"Marina del Rey\" brings in a familiar related name, and \"For all intensive purposes\" puts different words into a familiar expression, alongside the collected error \"It's in the stimulus.\"","children":["The attempt \"speech plath... speech plang\" for \"speech-language pathology\" keeps its **attempts and repairs**, which are part of the evidence."]}],"m1-lecture08-p016":[],"m1-lecture08-p017":[{"text":"The phrase \"you enter a hundred-dollar room and you do yoga\" was said about **hot yoga**, so the context is what makes it an error.","children":[]},{"text":"In a talk about cost the phrase would be ordinary, but for hot yoga the amount could concern the room's temperature, so dollar is a meaningful word in the **wrong relation to the message**.","children":[]},{"text":"The clause stays grammatical and the words come out clearly, so the error points to the **choice of concept and lemma**, not to phoneme ordering or articulation.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p018":[{"text":"In \"Law and Order SUV\", the title letters SVU come out as SUV, so the **same three letters** appear with the last two in a different order.","children":[]},{"text":"SUV is a **familiar vehicle abbreviation**, so a competing familiar form may have been selected, rather than every reordering being an isolated motor slip.","children":[]},{"text":"The utterance is still easy to pronounce, so the analysis starts from the SVU and SUV pattern and asks which representations hold each sequence.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p019":[{"text":"\"He cooked his kitchen\" was said when the speaker meant chicken.","children":[]},{"text":"Chicken and kitchen **share much of their sound structure**, and their first and middle consonants sit in opposite positions.","children":[]},{"text":"Both words also relate to **cooking**, so **sound similarity** and **meaning in context** come together in this one error.","children":["Kitchen still fits the noun slot after his, so the grammatical frame survives and the error lies in selecting and ordering the word form."]}],"m1-lecture08-p020":[{"text":"**Observational studies** of speech errors depend on **what the observer listens for**, and reading about speech production can change those **expectations** over time.","children":[]},{"text":"Those expectations can affect **what is heard** and **what is reported**, because someone has to notice a sound, treat it as an error, remember its wording and describe it.","children":[]},{"text":"Placing an error in the model also takes facts about the **situation**, such as the intended message, so context is needed to reconstruct how the error happened.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p021":[{"text":"**Speech perception** shapes which errors observers report, because people are not very good at hearing sounds **halfway between two phonemes**, especially consonants.","children":["People are also **biased against phonotactically illegal strings** and **biased toward hearing words**, so a sound partway between \"giss\" and \"kiss\" sounds more like \"kiss\"."]},{"text":"This is **theory-dependent observation**, so observers might miss many errors, or whole classes of errors, because of the way perception works.","children":[]},{"text":"**Experimental methods** and **careful measurement** can fix some of these problems, but far from all of them.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p022":[],"m1-lecture08-p023":[],"m1-lecture09-p001":[],"m1-lecture09-p002":[{"text":"**Oldfield and Wingfield** asked people to name pictures of objects, a task called **picture naming**, and timed how long each name took.","children":["The example line drawings come from **Snodgrass and Vanderwart**, a later set of drawings, and not from the original study."]},{"text":"They found a **log-linear relationship** between **word frequency** and **response time**, so more frequent names were produced faster, and the relation is close to a straight line when frequency is put on a logarithmic scale.","children":[]},{"text":"The catch is that **frequency is related to word length**, even in a tiny sample of names, because frequent words tend to be shorter.","children":["So frequency and length change together and either one could explain the faster naming, which makes length a **confound** (another variable that changes along with the one being tested)."]}],"m1-lecture09-p003":[{"text":"A **lexical database** gathers information about many words in one place so the words can be searched and compared.","children":[]},{"text":"The **English Lexicon Project** gives the properties of words along with behavioral data from **naming** and **lexical decision** tasks, so researchers can measure their candidate words instead of trusting their intuitions about which words are common or similar.","children":["It has separate links for generating word and nonword lists and for getting trial-level naming and lexical decision data."]},{"text":"Measuring the words before choosing them makes **correlations among word properties** visible, such as the link between frequency and length, before those correlations end up in the conditions.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p004":[{"text":"The word-query tools give two ways to get lexical information about words.","children":[]},{"text":"A researcher can **generate a list of items with specific lexical characteristics**, which helps when a study needs words within particular ranges of frequency or length.","children":[]},{"text":"A researcher can also **submit a list of items to retrieve their lexical characteristics**, which helps when the words were already chosen for another reason, such as belonging to one semantic category.","children":["In both cases the measurements can reveal **unintended differences between conditions**, so they can be fixed before the study is run."]}],"m1-lecture09-p005":[{"text":"A database query separates the **fields to retrieve** from the **constraints** that decide which words enter the list.","children":[]},{"text":"**Length** and **frequency** can both be requested as output fields, while **minimum and maximum values** on a frequency measure restrict the range of words that come back.","children":["Getting both fields back lets the researcher check whether length changes along with frequency in the candidate words."]},{"text":"For a frequency comparison, the goal is items that **differ in frequency** while a likely confound such as **length** stays controlled.","children":["The database only supplies candidates, so the researcher still has to decide whether more selection is needed."]}],"m1-lecture09-p006":[{"text":"The two hundred most frequent words in an old corpus were sorted into ten **bins** of twenty words each, from most to least frequent.","children":[]},{"text":"The **mean frequency drops off very dramatically** from the most frequent bin to the next and keeps falling, which is **Zipf's law** in action, so a small number of words account for a large share of use.","children":[]},{"text":"**Length is inversely related to frequency**, so the most frequent bins hold the shortest words on average.","children":["Because of this, picking very frequent words automatically picks many short words, and a random set of low-frequency words will differ in length as well as in frequency."]}],"m1-lecture09-p007":[{"text":"**Boxplots** compare the lengths of the four hundred sixty-seven most frequent words, the **high-frequency (HF)** words, with the same number of **low-frequency (LF)** words selected at random.","children":["A boxplot gives the spread of a whole set, with the **median** marking the middle value and the box covering the middle half."]},{"text":"**Fewer than a quarter of HF words are as long as the median LF word**, so HF words are shorter across the whole set and not because of a few odd words.","children":[]},{"text":"Picking LF words at random keeps the natural link between frequency and length, so random sampling does not remove the **length confound**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p008":[{"text":"The problem is that **HF words are generally shorter than LF words**, so choosing LF words at random will always give a length difference.","children":[]},{"text":"The solution is to **not select LF words at random**.","children":["Choosing **long-ish HF words** and **short-ish LF words** lets the two sets **match on length** while still **differing in frequency**."]},{"text":"Matching changes only the experimental sample, because frequency and length stay related in the language itself, and it lets the frequency difference be tested with length held similar.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p009":[{"text":"**Oldfield and Wingfield** eventually **matched their high- and low-frequency words for length**.","children":[]},{"text":"They still found a **robust frequency effect**, so they concluded that **frequency effects are not merely length effects in disguise**.","children":["If length were the whole cause, matching length should have removed the naming difference, and because the difference stayed, frequency matters beyond length."]},{"text":"A large share of psycholinguistics is an attempt to establish that an observed effect is **not just an effect of some other confounding variable**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p010":[{"text":"**Cutler** described the problem in the title \"Making up materials is a confounded nuisance,\" because good stimuli have to be **matched on many dimensions at once**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Cortese and Simpson** did a very careful matching job, with regular and irregular words matched on properties such as frequency, familiarity, imageability, length and phonological or orthographic measures.","children":[]},{"text":"Such fine-grained matching also has costs.","children":["The items may be **odd** or have inappropriate statistics, because the frequency and length confound is a real fact about English and most languages, so avoiding it creates a somewhat **artificial stimulus environment**.","Matching can also leave **very few items**, such as the ten words per cell in the study by Cortese and Simpson, which is small for a word naming experiment."]}],"m1-lecture09-p011":[{"text":"Most interesting effects in psycholinguistics happen **below the level of conscious awareness**, and that shapes **task design**.","children":[]},{"text":"A person who has not studied psycholinguistics would probably not think about **word frequency** or **length** while naming line drawings, so psycholinguists do not have to be as crafty in design as researchers who study racial stereotyping or altruism.","children":[]},{"text":"In **norming studies**, researchers routinely just ask people to **rate words** on some dimension and hope for the best.","children":["Norming studies can still include **fillers**, which are extra items mixed in among the critical ones to shape the task context."]}],"m1-lecture09-p012":[{"text":"Many tasks measure things that participants **do not consciously control**, and the tools range from the nineteenth-century **tachistoscope**, which controls brief presentation of stimuli, to eye-tracking, EEG and fMRI.","children":[]},{"text":"**Eye movements**, recorded with **eye-tracking**, can be very revealing, as in the speech production studies that follow.","children":["**Reaction time** and **accuracy** are also unlikely to be under direct control in most experiments."]},{"text":"**EEG** and **fMRI** both measure **brain activity**, which is useful mainly because it is a **dependent variable** (the measured outcome) that participants usually do not control directly.","children":["If different kinds of stimuli bring different brain responses even with **no overt task**, the stimuli are likely different to the participant."]}],"m1-lecture09-p013":[],"m1-lecture09-p014":[{"text":"**Belke, Meyer and Damian** used **cyclic semantic blocking** to study **picture naming**, in which participants named the same sets of objects again and again.","children":[]},{"text":"In **homogeneous** sets the names were semantically related, and in **heterogeneous** sets they were unrelated, and naming was **slower in related sets** once the objects had already been named once.","children":[]},{"text":"A later experiment showed the objects all at once and found blocking in **naming latency** and **gaze duration**, but no significant blocking in the lag between the eyes leaving an object and the start of its name.","children":["A further experiment found that the cost **generalized to new names** from the same categories."]}],"m1-lecture09-p015":[{"text":"The **WEAVER++** model from **Roelofs** splits word production into **conceptual**, **syntactic** and **word-form** levels, and it focuses on **activation for single words**.","children":[]},{"text":"Because of that focus, it does not line up exactly with the more general production model, which has message, functional, positional, phonological encoding and articulation levels.","children":["In WEAVER++ the syntactic information is not positional, because it covers single-word features such as **number** and **gender**."]},{"text":"The conceptual level and the **lemma** level (the stored word candidates) matter most for semantic blocking, because activation from a concept reaches the word candidates there, before their sounds are planned.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p016":[{"text":"A naive view of **spreading activation** predicts that a whole block of similar pictures should make naming **faster**, because activating one concept helps activate related ones.","children":["Duck connects through shared features such as animal, bird, swim and scales to fish, snake and mouse."]},{"text":"The model instead helps explain how spreading activation can make naming **slower** in some cases.","children":["A related set activates the target but also activates its **competitors**, and naming requires choosing just one candidate, so the extra activation can make the choice harder."]}],"m1-lecture09-p017":[{"text":"Speech production requires a careful distinction between **activation** and **selection**.","children":[]},{"text":"Spreading activation from duck to fish means that activating one helps activate the other, but this causes trouble when the task is to **select the correct lemma** from many active ones.","children":[]},{"text":"**WEAVER++** assumes that **selection time** depends on the **ratio** of the activity of the most active lemma to the **overall activation of all lemmas**.","children":["Feedback from the category level adds **collateral activation** to other items in the same category, so the target is a smaller share of the total and **lemma selection slows down**."]}],"m1-lecture09-p018":[{"text":"**Belke and colleagues** ran the experiment for eight **cycles**, and every stimulus was presented in each cycle.","children":[]},{"text":"**Homogeneous (HOM)** blocks held related items such as \"duck\", \"mouse\", \"fish\" and \"snake\", while **heterogeneous (HET)** blocks held unrelated items such as \"duck\", \"saw\", \"chair\" and \"bus\".","children":[]},{"text":"The same stimulus, such as \"duck\", appeared in HOM and HET blocks in **alternating cycles**, so each item could be compared across a related and an unrelated context.","children":["An item counts as HOM or HET only because of the other items named with it."]}],"m1-lecture09-p019":[{"text":"**Two effects** happen at the same time in the naming times.","children":[]},{"text":"A big **identity priming effect**, which is faster naming of an item that was named before, makes naming much faster after the first cycle in both HOM and HET blocks.","children":[]},{"text":"A **semantic blocking effect** makes naming **slower in HOM than in HET** blocks once the objects have been named.","children":["The two do not conflict, because priming is a change across repeated encounters with an item, while blocking is a difference between related and unrelated contexts at the same point."]}],"m1-lecture09-p020":[{"text":"One competing explanation is that the **blocking effect arises at the level of visual object recognition**, because objects in the same category might look alike.","children":[]},{"text":"The first response is that the stimuli were **normed** so that objects in the same category were **not more visually similar** on average than objects in different categories.","children":[]},{"text":"The second response is a **control experiment** in which **visual similarity** was blocked instead, and it gave **different results**, because that blocking effect appeared in the **first block of trials**.","children":["The semantic effect appears only after the first cycle, so the effects in the first experiment are **not likely due to visual similarity**."]}],"m1-lecture09-p021":[{"text":"A study by **Hermens and colleagues** showed people several objects **simultaneously** and found no effect, which raises the worry that blocking depends on seeing objects **one at a time**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Belke and colleagues** tested this by having participants view **displays of four items at once** and produce their names **in serial**, one after another.","children":["A display of duck, snake, fish and mouse is homogeneous, because all four are animals."]},{"text":"This keeps seeing simultaneous while keeping speaking serial, so it tests whether semantic competition remains when the next objects are already in view.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p022":[{"text":"**Belke and colleagues** **measured eye movements** while participants named the four objects in order.","children":[]},{"text":"**Eye tracking** records how long the eyes stay on each object, the **gaze duration** (GD), and the onset of articulation (Art) marks when each name begins.","children":["The eyes move on to the next object before the name of the previous one is spoken, so looking runs ahead of speaking."]},{"text":"This divides each naming delay into separate parts, so each part can be compared across HOM and HET displays.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p023":[{"text":"There are two key **dependent variables** in the eye-tracking experiment.","children":[]},{"text":"**Gaze duration** is how long the eyes stay on an object, and there is evidence that **lemma selection is coordinated with eye movements**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Lag** is the time between the eyes leaving an object and the start of its name.","children":["Later processes such as **phonological encoding** (planning the sounds of the word) and **gesture planning** (planning the speech movements) can happen **without visual support** once the eyes have moved on.","So a semantic effect on gaze duration points to selection, while an effect on lag would point to these later processes."]}],"m1-lecture09-p024":[{"text":"**Griffin, Bock and colleagues** made demonstrations of **eye-mouth coordination**, which follows where a speaker looks while describing an event.","children":[]},{"text":"One example is correctly naming the **patient** in \"The horse is kicking a cow.\"","children":["The horse is the **agent** (the one doing the action), and the cow is the **patient** (the one the action affects)."]},{"text":"Lining up the eye record with the spoken words reveals when information about each participant in the event becomes relevant to the sentence.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p025":[{"text":"In the eye-tracking experiment, **overall speech duration decreases** over the course of the experiment but has **no blocking effect**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Gaze duration has a blocking effect**, with longer looking in HOM displays, but **only after the first cycle**.","children":[]},{"text":"There is **no significant effect on lag**.","children":["Numerically the blocking effect on lag seems to go in the **opposite direction**, but this difference is **not significant**, so the semantic cost appears clearly only in gaze duration."]}],"m1-lecture09-p026":[{"text":"Each measure is linked to a production stage, so **speech duration** reflects the **gesture level**, **gaze duration** reflects **lemma selection**, and **lag** reflects **phonological encoding**.","children":[]},{"text":"Only gaze duration has a blocking effect after the first cycle, so the result supports a **lemma-selection account**, in which related lemmas compete longer in HOM sets.","children":[]},{"text":"Speech duration and lag have no significant blocking effect, so they give no matching evidence for a blocking cost at the gesture level or in phonological encoding.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p027":[{"text":"**Experiment three** asked whether **blocking generalizes to new items** in the same category.","children":[]},{"text":"New items were introduced in the **fifth cycle**, and the **repetition priming effect went away** there, so overall naming **slowed down dramatically**.","children":["Even so, the **blocking effect was still there**, with HOM slower than HET."]},{"text":"Comparing the new items from a blocked category with the items in the **first cycle** helps separate experience with a category from having named one particular object before.","children":["The first cycle has no blocking, while new items from an already blocked category do, so the effect is tied to the category and not to repeating specific items."]}],"m1-lecture09-p028":[{"text":"**Belke and colleagues** isolate the blocking effect in the model by ruling out three other sources.","children":[]},{"text":"It is **not visual recognition**, because of the stimulus norming and the visual-similarity control experiment.","children":[]},{"text":"It is **not phonological encoding**, because the words are not similar in sound, and it is **not item-specific**, because it **generalizes to new items**.","children":["What remains is **lemma selection** among semantically activated competitors."]}],"m1-lecture09-p029":[{"text":"The **visual recognition** explanation is ruled out by **normed and matched stimuli** and by the **control experiment** on visual similarity.","children":[]},{"text":"**Phonological encoding** is ruled out more tentatively, because the words are **not phonologically similar** and the effect is in **gaze duration, not eye-speech lag**.","children":["This one carries a question mark, because the timing measures support an interpretation of a hidden stage and do not observe that stage directly."]},{"text":"The effect is **not just a specific effect of repeating lists**, because it **generalizes to new items** from the same categories.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p030":[{"text":"A good way to get an idea for an experiment is to ask whether an effect \"couldn't just be\" something else.","children":[]},{"text":"For **stimulus manipulations**, many features of words are **correlated with frequency**, including **length**, **age of acquisition**, **concreteness or imageability** and **contextual diversity**.","children":["So frequency effects might be just a **catch-all** for these other properties."]},{"text":"For **experimental manipulations**, even the simple blocking study involves several processes, such as **object recognition** and **response selection**, and each one could explain an effect.","children":["A design then tests each alternative by matching it, manipulating it directly, or adding a new measure."]}],"m1-lecture09-p031":[],"m1-review-p001":[],"m1-review-p002":[{"text":"The exam has **ten short essay questions**, and roughly one hundred to two hundred fifty words should be enough to answer each one.","children":[]},{"text":"Each question holds **three or four gradeable items**, so there is a lot of **partial credit** available for explaining the parts you understand well.","children":[]},{"text":"The exam is **open book**, so there is no need to memorize names and dates, and preparation is better spent on explaining concepts and connecting each finding to the claim it supports.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p003":[],"m1-review-p004":[{"text":"Consonants are organized by three separate features, and each one answers a different question about how the sound is made.","children":[]},{"text":"**Place of articulation** is where the vocal tract is narrowed or closed, running from the front of the vocal tract to the back across the columns of the IPA consonant chart.","children":["**Manner of articulation** is the degree of closure, running from closed to open down the rows, so a **plosive** closes completely while a **fricative** leaves a narrow passage."]},{"text":"**Voicing** is whether the vocal folds start vibrating before or during the closure or only after it.","children":["Voiceless and voiced sounds sit in pairs at the same place and manner, with the voiced one on the right, so two sounds can share two features and differ only in the third."]}],"m1-review-p005":[{"text":"/p/ and /b/ are both **plosive** and both **bilabial**, so they have the same manner and place of articulation.","children":[]},{"text":"They differ in **voicing**, which is the timing of **phonation**, the vibration of the vocal folds, relative to the release of the lip closure.","children":["So /p/ is marked as minus voiced and /b/ as plus voiced, and features describe their similarity and their difference in a precise, well-defined way."]},{"text":"The same voicing difference separates /t/ from /d/, so we can talk in general about **the voiced stops** /b/, /d/, /g/ and so on, which is helpful for **phonological descriptions**.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p006":[{"text":"A **minimal pair** is two words that differ in only one sound, where that single difference changes the meaning. Having two different low back vowels was established by finding two words whose distinction depended only on those vowels, such as caught and cot for a speaker who keeps them apart.","children":[]},{"text":"If two **phones** are **contrastive**, meaning there is a minimal pair where swapping them changes the meaning, then they are examples of **different phonemes**.","children":["The evidence is the change in meaning, not just the fact that two pronunciations sound different."]}],"m1-review-p007":[{"text":"Minimal pairs also give evidence that a phonemic contrast depends on a particular **feature**. In English, /b/ and /p/, /t/ and /d/, and /k/ and /g/ all differ on **voicing**, and this is known from comparing minimal pairs for each of them.","children":["Each pair keeps the same place and manner and changes only voicing, so one feature is doing the same job at three different places."]},{"text":"This evidence has to come from minimal pairs **in each language**, because two sounds that contrast in one language may not contrast in another.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p008":[{"text":"Phonemes are not letters, because speech is not produced as an **orderly stream of letter-like units**, even though literate people find it easy to imagine it that way.","children":[]},{"text":"A **spectrogram** of \"A sore back is a real pain\" plots time from left to right and frequency from bottom to top, with darker areas showing stronger energy. The sounds **blend and overlap** continuously, so there are no clean breaks where one phoneme ends and the next begins.","children":["A phonemic transcription gives useful categories, but each category does not match a separate block of sound with the same shape in every context."]}],"m1-review-p009":[{"text":"**Hockett** compared speech to a row of unboiled Easter eggs of various sizes and colors carried on a moving belt through a **wringer** that smashes them and rubs them into each other.","children":[]},{"text":"The eggs going in stand for the impulses from the **phoneme source**, and the mess coming out stands for the output of the **speech transmitter**, so neighboring sounds smear together.","children":[]},{"text":"The **hearer** is an inspector who examines the mess and decides which eggs went in, using the broken yolks, spread albumen and bits of shell.","children":["The inspector does not have to put the eggs back together, which is impossible, but only has to **identify** them, so hearing speech means identifying the units rather than rebuilding them."]}],"m1-review-p010":[],"m1-review-p011":[{"text":"**Derivational morphology** is a process that derives new words from existing parts, and those parts are called **morphemes**.","children":[]},{"text":"The word morphology is built this way, from morph-, from the Greek for form, plus -ology, roughly the study of, so it means **the study of the forms of words**.","children":["A similar process gives morpheme, and phoneme is built on phone, another Greek morpheme, for speech."]},{"text":"Asking what to call a unit in text, and what graphology means, pushes the same pattern further, and the joking \"oops\" hints that a word built from familiar parts does not always mean what its parts suggest.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p012":[{"text":"Derivational morphology is **productive, but in limited ways**, both in how widely and in how regularly an affix is applied. The suffix **-hood** added to a noun stem gives a state of being tied to that noun, as in brotherhood, sisterhood and knighthood.","children":["Even these differ, because two brothers share their brotherhood while each knight has his own knighthood."]},{"text":"Neighborhood is a perfectly good word, but -hood does a different job in it entirely. Friendhood is not used because English says friendship for no apparent reason, and enemyhood is missing, so the idea needs words like enmity or antagonism.","children":["Several of these examples come from **Liberman and Prince**."]}],"m1-review-p013":[{"text":"**Zero derivation** turns a word into another category without adding any affix, yet it still does **complicated things with meaning**.","children":[]},{"text":"Dish, spoon, fork and table are strong associates of the noun knife, but their meanings as verbs are **not similar**. In \"She can dish it out but she can't take it\", \"a forked tongue\" and \"table the issue\", each word carries a conventional meaning that does not follow simply from the object.","children":["So knowing the noun does not predict what the converted form means, because each use has to be learned."]},{"text":"An expression like \"silver spoon\" is the kind of figurative meaning studied in **Cognitive Linguistics**.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p014":[{"text":"More commonly, derivational morphology **changes the pronunciation** and spelling of a word, for example by adding affixes. In certificate, certification and recertification, each added affix moves the **stress** and changes vowels in the rest of the word.","children":["This is one reason for \"The Chaos\" of English spelling, and it is why spelling closer to the spoken form does not necessarily make reading easier."]},{"text":"The noun and verb forms of record and invite differ mainly in **stress**, as in \"He did not receive an invite\" and \"We did not invite him\".","children":["The two forms of entrance also differ in stress, but they are not actually related to each other."]}],"m1-review-p015":[{"text":"**Inflectional morphology** changes the meaning of words in a more **predictable** way than derivation does. It marks **plurals** such as knives, forks and mice, the **third person singular present** such as walks, runs and the verb knifes, and the **past** such as walked, sang and knifed.","children":[]},{"text":"**Menn** says it would be silly to have separate dictionary entries for the singular and plural forms of nouns, because they are one word in different grammatical forms.","children":[]},{"text":"The sound changes in inflection often have some **irregularity**, as mice and sang show.","children":["That irregularity matters later for **language learning models** and for kinds of **developmental language impairment**."]}],"m1-review-p016":[{"text":"Inflectional morphology also **depends on the syntax** of the rest of the sentence. In the phrase \"two [noun]+pl\", whatever noun goes there must be **pluralized** somehow, whether regularly as in forks or irregularly as in knives.","children":[]},{"text":"There are **no syntactic processes like this** for even very productive derivational forms. The closest is a formula like \"Once you [verb] it, you can un-[verb] it\", but that is not really syntax, because no sentence forces a verb into a derived form.","children":["This difference between required inflection and optional derivation matters for **models of speech production**."]}],"m1-review-p017":[],"m1-review-p018":[{"text":"A very simple model treats each word as a **node** in a **lexical network**, a unit that can be activated by a specific word. Here the network has three nodes, for lion, tiger and stripes.","children":[]},{"text":"When the printed word LION appears, it supplies evidence that activates the lion node.","children":["The printed input and the node are different levels, because the input is what is seen and the node is the stored word that the system can recognize."]}],"m1-review-p019":[{"text":"Activation in this network **can be partial**, so a node can be somewhat active before its word is recognized.","children":[]},{"text":"When the word LION **contrasts poorly with the noise mask**, the evidence for it is weak, so the lion node gets only **weak activation**.","children":["The system builds toward a decision as evidence emerges from the noise, instead of switching straight from off to on."]}],"m1-review-p020":[{"text":"When the **signal-to-noise ratio** becomes very favorable, the word stands out clearly from the noise, so activation of the lion node becomes **strong**. At that point this simple network has **identified** the word lion.","children":[]},{"text":"This is the **perceptual identification task**, in which participants push a button as soon as they can identify the item.","children":["The button press times identification, so conditions that make identification easier lead to earlier responses."]}],"m1-review-p021":[{"text":"Most models of this kind assume that **activation unfolds over time**, rising after the input arrives and then falling.","children":[]},{"text":"One way to explain **frequency effects**, the faster identification of common words, is that experience tunes these **activation dynamics**. Every time you meet a word, it might raise its **baseline activation**, **strengthen incoming connections** so it activates more quickly, or change a **threshold** for recognition.","children":["This is a very simple form of **learning**."]},{"text":"Different computational models, such as **Rumelhart and McClelland's Interactive Activation model**, simulate frequency effects in different ways.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p022":[{"text":"Activation dynamics also explain **repetition priming**, where seeing a word again very shortly after seeing it leads to **faster identification**.","children":[]},{"text":"Activation does not rise and fall instantly, and it may **fall more slowly than it rises**. So if the word appears again while activation is still high from the last exposure, its starting level is already **higher than usual**, and it reaches identification sooner.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p023":[{"text":"Two factors need something added to the simple network before it can explain them. **Neighborhood effects** depend on how similar a word is to other words, and **associative, semantic or phonological priming** depends on whether you just saw a similar or related word.","children":[]},{"text":"A network of isolated nodes can explain changes in one word's own activity, but these effects involve one word changing another, so the model needs **connections between nodes**.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p024":[{"text":"**Spreading activation** is one way to represent the associations measured in **association norms**. Words sit in a network where the **connection strength**, or distance, between two nodes is set by how strongly they are associated.","children":[]},{"text":"So when you activate lion, some of that activation travels along a **weighted connection** to tiger.","children":["Tiger is then partly active before it appears, which is how the network explains **semantic priming**."]}],"m1-review-p025":[{"text":"**Balota and Lorch** asked whether activation automatically spreads **beyond directly associated concepts**. They built prime and target pairs related only through a **mediating concept**, such as the prime lion and the target stripes, which connect only through tiger.","children":[]},{"text":"In **lexical decision**, where people judge whether the target is a word, directly related pairs like lion and tiger or tiger and stripes sped responses, but lion and stripes **did not help** compared with a neutral or unrelated prime.","children":[]},{"text":"In **pronunciation**, where people read the target aloud, both the directly related pairs and pairs like lion and stripes **sped responses**.","children":["This second benefit is **mediated priming**, the priming of a target through a word that is linked to both the prime and the target.","They concluded that activation spreads beyond direct associates, and that features of the lexical decision task **masked** the mediated priming effect."]}],"m1-review-p026":[{"text":"Activity flows throughout the network, so activating lion can prime **stripes** even though the two words are **not directly associated**.","children":[]},{"text":"Lion activates tiger, and tiger passes some activation on to stripes, so stripes gets a head start through the shared link. This is called **mediated priming**, because the prime reaches the target through a linking word, here tiger.","children":["A direct association works in one step, while this kind of priming needs activation to travel two steps through the network."]}],"m1-review-p027":[{"text":"In pronunciation, directly related primes gave the fastest responses, primes in the **mediated priming** condition, such as lion before stripes, were a little slower, and neutral and unrelated primes were slowest.","children":["This held at both **stimulus onset asynchronies**, the short and the longer delay between the start of the prime and the start of the target."]},{"text":"The benefit through tiger was only a small fraction of a second, so this study is typical of psycholinguistics, with **careful study design** and **small effects**.","children":[]},{"text":"The result is also **counterintuitive**, because lion and stripes seem unrelated. Even so, reading lion first speeds up saying stripes, because both words are associated with tiger.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p028":[{"text":"**Belke and colleagues** studied a case where spreading activation **slows** naming down. A naive view of spreading activation predicts that a whole block of similar pictures presented together would lead to **faster naming**, because each picture would pre-activate the others.","children":[]},{"text":"In the model, duck, fish, snake and mouse share concepts and features such as animal, bird, swim and scales, and each concept connects to its own **lemma**, the word candidate.","children":["Activating one animal also activates its competitors, so choosing one lemma among several active ones takes longer, which leads to **slower naming times**."]}],"m1-review-p029":[],"m1-review-p030":[{"text":"The neurologist **Pierre Paul Broca** presented the case of **Tan**, who had lost the ability to speak after a brain injury decades earlier. He gave the presentation to the newly established Society of Anthropology, where he was also the founding president.","children":[]},{"text":"Tan could produce only **one jargon syllable**, yet he seemed otherwise in possession of his senses and seemed to **understand** what was being said.","children":["This split between lost speaking and kept understanding suggested that producing and understanding speech can be damaged separately, although it was not the first observation of such a specific loss."]}],"m1-review-p031":[{"text":"Tan's brain, on display at the Musee Dupuytren, had a **large lesion** that definitely included the third convolution of the frontal lobe, the **inferior frontal gyrus**.","children":[]},{"text":"From this came **Broca's area**, a portion of the left frontal lobe that is important for speech.","children":[]},{"text":"**Hughlings Jackson** objected that locating the damage that destroys speech and **locating speech** are two different things.","children":["Damage can stop speech by disrupting one part of a larger system, so the lesion site alone does not reveal where speech itself happens."]}],"m1-review-p032":[{"text":"In the eighteen-seventies, German neurologists such as **Lichtheim** and **Wernicke** began making more systematic observations of **aphasia**, language loss after brain damage. They ascribed specific losses of function to **disconnections** between **centers**.","children":[]},{"text":"In Lichtheim's model, A holds sound images, B holds concepts and M holds movement images. A disconnection between A and B could cause a loss of understanding spoken words, and a disconnection between A and M could cause a loss of **verbatim repetition**.","children":["Describing centers for specific functions is an ancestor of the later idea of **modularity**."]}],"m1-review-p033":[{"text":"**Freud** criticized the \"diagram-drawing\" school of aphasia research in his early neurological work. He felt it was **naive** to expect the brain to be organized by psychological categories like word forms or concepts.","children":[]},{"text":"He proposed instead that language emerged from **fields** connecting perceptual and motor abilities to **drives**, which makes his view a **holist critique**.","children":[]},{"text":"This leaves a durable tension in **cognitive neuroscience** over whether brain area functions and cognitive functions can be directly related.","children":["A psychological category can be useful without matching one brain area, so whether the brain is organized by our descriptions has to be tested with evidence rather than assumed."]}],"m1-review-p034":[{"text":"**Dronkers and colleagues** used **MRI** to assess the extent and location of damage in Broca's landmark cases very precisely, including the brain of **Leborgne**, the patient known as Tan.","children":[]},{"text":"The lesion is very **deep** and includes the **insula**, with a lot of white matter damage to the **superior longitudinal fasciculus**, which connects the front and back of the cerebral cortex.","children":[]},{"text":"Other work by this group suggests that the **insula**, not Broca's area, is strongly associated with **speech apraxia**, the condition Broca described in his patients.","children":["So the damage was far larger than a small surface speech center, which changes how the classic cases should be read."]}],"m1-review-p035":[],"m1-review-p036":[{"text":"At the **functional level** we select **lemmas** rather than finished words, and the term lemma is borrowed from mathematics, where it is a proposition in an argument.","children":[]},{"text":"In the error \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in\" for the intended \"Where'd I put it when I brought it in\", the verbs switched places, but the produced words reflect a **tense change** applied later.","children":["This could not have happened if the word brought had been selected, because brought would have moved unchanged."]},{"text":"So the speaker selected the lemma **BRING**, which carries no tense yet, and it was later expressed as **present tense** in its new position.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p037":[{"text":"People so often say the **opposite** of what they mean because **antonyms** are similar at several levels. They are **semantically related** and **strongly associated**, which makes a perfect storm in the **spreading activation** framework.","children":[]},{"text":"Antonyms can also take on the **same semantic roles** and belong to the **same word class**, so they can fill the same syntactic roles.","children":["That means the rest of the speech production system can handle the wrong word without trouble."]},{"text":"So activation spreads easily from a lemma to its antonym, and if the antonym wins, the sentence still comes out grammatical.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p038":[{"text":"**Semantic roles**, also called functional roles, describe how each participant relates to the event. In \"The plums in the icebox were eaten by me\", the plums are the **Undergoer**, in the icebox is the **Location**, and me is the **Agent**.","children":[]},{"text":"\"I have eaten the plums in the icebox\" gives the same roles in a different order, with the Agent first, then the verb, the Undergoer and the Location.","children":["With this simple event structure, lemmas can be slotted into roles, so TO EAT is the verb, ME is the Agent and PLUM is the Undergoer."]},{"text":"Different orders have **syntactic consequences**, including whether the lemma ME becomes I or me, and that is worked out at the **positional level**.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p039":[{"text":"By the end of the **functional level**, the speaker has decided on a **message** to convey, selected the appropriate **lemmas**, and associated those lemmas with **semantic roles**.","children":[]},{"text":"That is quite a lot of work, yet the speaker is still nowhere near **putting a clause together**, much less saying it out loud.","children":["For an eating event, the plan knows EAT, who eats and what is eaten, but not yet whether the sentence is active or passive, how pronouns are expressed, or how it sounds."]}],"m1-review-p040":[{"text":"For \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in?\", the **functional level** has already supplied two verbs, PUT and BRING, with I or ME as the **Agent** of both and IT as the **Undergoer** of both.","children":[]},{"text":"What is still missing is the **temporal relationship** between the verbs, which is how the bringing and the putting relate in time, along with the question structure and the tense of each verb.","children":["Expressing all of these things syntactically is the job of the **positional level**, which turns the roles into clauses and grammatical positions."]}],"m1-review-p041":[{"text":"Setting aside how the question is formed and how the tenses are worked out, the **positional level** produces a frame like \"Where'd I [verb, root form] it when I [verb, past] it in?\"","children":["The first clause takes a lot of syntax, because forming the question needs both **Wh- movement** and an **auxiliary verb**."]},{"text":"The first clause requires the **base form** of the verb and the next clause requires the **past form**. So the positional level is a **syntactic structure with slots** for the lemmas from the functional level, plus the other particles the grammar requires.","children":[]},{"text":"The error \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in\" arises while these **slots are being filled** with lemmas, so each swapped verb takes the form of the slot it lands in.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p042":[{"text":"The error \"a language needer learns\" is similar to the bring and put error, because **two lemmas got switched** as they were fed into the positional level while the **positional level stayed largely the same**.","children":[]},{"text":"It differs in the noun, which is actually a **verb plus an affix**, so this error involves **derivational morphology**.","children":["The noun slot was built as a verb plus -er, so the swapped root need became needer, while the verb slot kept its third person ending and gave learns.","So the roots moved and the affixes stayed where the structure put them, which makes them **stranded affixes**."]}],"m1-review-p043":[{"text":"\"He did that easy enoughly\" is similar to \"a language needer learns\" because both contain a **stranded affix**. Here the affix is **-ly**, which turns an adjective into an adverb.","children":[]},{"text":"It differs because something also happened while **setting up the positional level**, not only while filling its slots.","children":["In the faulty structure the adverb position, built as an adjective plus -ly, comes after the adjective position, so -ly lands on enough and easy is left without its ending."]}],"m1-review-p044":[],"m1-review-p045":[{"text":"**Oldfield and Wingfield** asked people to **name pictures** of objects and found a **log-linear relationship** between frequency and response time, so naming time dropped steadily as the logarithm of word frequency rose.","children":["The line drawings come from **Snodgrass and Vanderwart**."]},{"text":"This is a classic cautionary tale, because **frequency is related to word length**, even in this tiny sample.","children":["Since frequent words tend to be shorter, frequency and length change together, so either one could explain the faster naming, which makes length a **confound**."]}],"m1-review-p046":[{"text":"Taking the two hundred most frequent words in an old corpus and putting them into ten **bins** of twenty words each reveals two patterns.","children":[]},{"text":"First, **mean frequency drops off very dramatically** from the most frequent bin to the next most frequent and so on, which is **Zipf's law** in action.","children":[]},{"text":"Second, **length is inversely related to frequency**, so the highest-frequency bins hold the shortest words on average.","children":["That is why choosing very frequent words automatically means choosing many short words."]}],"m1-review-p047":[{"text":"High-frequency words are generally **shorter** than low-frequency words, so a set of low-frequency words selected at random will always end up longer on average.","children":[]},{"text":"The solution is to **not select low-frequency words at random**. Choosing longish high-frequency words and shortish low-frequency words lets you **match on length** while still **manipulating frequency**.","children":["Matching changes only the experimental sample, not the language, and the final groups must still differ in frequency while agreeing in length."]}],"m1-review-p048":[{"text":"**Oldfield and Wingfield** eventually **matched** their high-frequency and low-frequency words **for length** and still found a **robust frequency effect**.","children":[]},{"text":"They concluded that frequency effects are **not merely length effects** in disguise, because if length were the whole story, matching it would have removed the difference.","children":[]},{"text":"Rather a lot of psycholinguistics is an attempt to prove that some observed effect is **not just an effect of a confounding variable**.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p049":[],"m1-review-p050":[{"text":"**Belke, Meyer and Damian** used **cyclic semantic blocking**, in which participants repeatedly name sets of objects with **semantically related names**, called homogeneous sets, or unrelated names, called heterogeneous sets. Naming is typically **slower in related sets**.","children":["Their first experiment found the blocking effect only after all objects of a set had been shown and named once.","Their second showed a set's objects at the same time and found blocking in **naming latencies** and **gaze durations**, but the **gaze-to-speech lag** showed repetition priming and no blocking.","Their third found that blocking **generalized to new**, previously unnamed items."]},{"text":"They proposed that the blocking effect is due to **refractory behaviour in the semantic system**.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p051":[{"text":"In speech production, **activation** and **selection** must be kept carefully apart. Spreading activation from duck to fish means that activating one can help activate the other, but that causes problems when the task is to **select the correct lemma** from a bunch of active ones.","children":[]},{"text":"The **WEAVER++** model assumes that **selection time** depends on the **ratio** of the activity of the most active lemma to the overall activation of all lemmas.","children":["So when feedback from the **category level** adds **collateral activation** to items from the same category, the target's share shrinks and **lemma selection slows down**."]}],"m1-review-p052":[{"text":"In the **cyclic semantic blocking** study by **Belke, Meyer and Damian**, people named the same sets of pictures again and again, either in **homogeneous sets** of related names or **heterogeneous sets** of unrelated names.","children":[]},{"text":"Naming took **longer in homogeneous sets**, but only once every object in the set had been named once.","children":["When all objects appeared together, related sets lengthened both **naming latencies** and **gaze durations**, while the lag between gaze leaving an object and speech starting showed **no blocking**.","The cost also carried over to **new items** from the same categories."]},{"text":"The authors traced the effect to **refractory behaviour in the semantic system**.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p053":[{"text":"Speech production needs a clear line between **activation**, which spreads from duck to fish and other related animals, and **selection**, which picks the one lemma that will be spoken.","children":[]},{"text":"In **WEAVER++**, **selection time** is set by the **ratio** of the most active lemma's activity to the total activation of all lemmas.","children":["**Category-level feedback** gives extra **collateral activation** to every member of the category, so the target stands out less and selecting it **takes longer**."]},{"text":"This is how related words that help each other's activation can still **slow down naming**.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p054":[{"text":"The second experiment tracked three measures across presentation cycles for homogeneous and heterogeneous sets.","children":[]},{"text":"**Speech duration** decreased over the course of the experiment but showed **no blocking effect**, and it is linked to the **gesture level**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Gaze duration** showed a **blocking effect** only **after the first cycle**, with longer gazes in homogeneous sets, and it is linked to **lemma selection**.","children":["There was **no effect on lag**, which is linked to **phonological encoding**, and the numerical trend in the opposite direction was not significant.","So the clearest cost falls at lemma selection, where competition among related lemmas happens."]}],"m1-review-p055":[{"text":"The third experiment asked whether **blocking generalizes to new items** in the same category, so new items were introduced in the fifth cycle.","children":[]},{"text":"With the new items the **repetition priming** benefit went away and performance **slowed down dramatically**, yet homogeneous sets stayed slower, so **the blocking effect was still there**.","children":["Comparing the new items from a blocked category to the items in the first cycle separates experience with the category from having named that exact object before.","In the first cycle homogeneous sets were not slower, yet the new items from an already named category were, so the cost comes from the category and not from the item."]},{"text":"So blocking is **not just a specific effect of repeating lists**, because it carries over to new members of a category.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p056":[{"text":"**Belke and colleagues** isolated the blocking effect by ruling out three alternatives. It is **not visual recognition**, because the stimuli were normed and matched and a **control experiment** was run.","children":[]},{"text":"It is probably **not phonological encoding**, because the words were **not phonologically similar** and **gaze duration**, not the eye-speech lag, was affected.","children":["This point carries a question mark because the timing measures support the interpretation without observing the stage directly."]},{"text":"It is **not just a specific effect of repeating lists**, because the effect **generalizes to new items**, which leaves **lemma selection** as the stage where blocking arises.","children":[]}]}};
